<?xml version='1.0' standalone='yes'?>
<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN [
<!ENTITY name    "steward">
<!ENTITY author  "hom-wang">
<!ENTITY version "2026.10.26">
<!ENTITY launch  "Settings/Steward">
]>

<!--
  Steward: runs Claude Code as an always-on service on the Unraid host, operated from a phone or browser
  through Remote Control. Independent of the array and Docker. Requires Unraid 7.3 or later with
  Internal Boot (/boot on ZFS); USB boot is not supported.

  Steward is an independent, community-maintained project. It is not affiliated with, endorsed by,
  or sponsored by Anthropic PBC or Lime Technology, Inc. "Claude" and "Claude Code" are trademarks of
  Anthropic PBC; "Unraid" is a trademark of Lime Technology, Inc. Steward downloads the unmodified
  Claude Code binary from Anthropic's release server, pinned to one version and checked against its published SHA256, and runs it as a background service; you sign
  in with your own Claude subscription through Anthropic's own login flow, Steward never sees, stores,
  or forwards your credentials, and all usage is billed to your own account.

  Install: copy this file to the server, then in the webGUI go to Plugins -> Install Plugin, enter its
           path and click Install, or run in a terminal:  plugin install /path/to/steward.plg
  Afterwards: Settings -> Steward. Data lives in /boot/steward; removing the plugin does not delete it.
  Upgrading from the old name claude-unraid: just install this plugin. Data is migrated automatically
  and the old plugin is torn down.

  The whole plugin is in this one file. In order below: the README, the settings page, the settings
  page's backend, the main script, the optional skills, and the steps run on install and removal.
-->

<PLUGIN name="&name;" author="&author;" version="&version;" launch="&launch;" icon="terminal" min="7.3.0" pluginURL="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/hom-wang/steward/main/steward.plg" support="https://github.com/hom-wang/steward/issues">

<CHANGES>
### 2026.10.26
- Optional skills for Claude, installed only on request with "steward skills install NAME": unraid (operating guide), unraid-docs (how to find correct information for the installed version), unraid-tools (which commands exist on Unraid), unraid-health (read-only health check). Each records the Unraid version it was verified on. A fresh install still ships none of them.
- The locale export is en_US.UTF-8, the only UTF-8 locale Unraid ships; upgrading from claude-unraid also removes the old plugin's RAM marker.

### 2026.10.25.1
- First install no longer runs Anthropic's install.sh. Steward downloads one pinned Claude Code version straight from the release server and installs it only if its SHA256 matches the value built into the plugin. The backup on the boot device is checked the same way before it is restored.
- After an automatic or manual update, the binary Claude Code's own updater installed is checked against the SHA256 Anthropic publishes for that version; a mismatch removes it, keeps the previous version and sends a notification.
- The listing and README state that claude-unraid is this plugin's earlier name and that installing over it migrates the data and removes the old plugin.

### 2026.10.25
- Everything is in English now: script messages and status output, the settings page, the default steward.conf and CLAUDE.md templates, bash-guard prompts, and this changelog. Existing installs keep their own steward.conf, CLAUDE.md, settings.json and hook untouched.
- Published at https://github.com/hom-wang/steward; the plugin now checks that repository for updates (pluginURL).

### 2026.10.24
- Auto-update: Claude Code does not update itself in remote-control mode, so the watchdog now checks the stable channel once a day after 08:00. A new version is installed and backed up to the boot device; the service restarts to apply it only when no session is in use, otherwise it retries an hour later. An Unraid notification is sent when done.
- Defaults for new installs: bash-guard hook (inspects the whole command: disk-wiping tools and secret paths are always blocked; deletion, shutdown, writes to /boot/config, git rewriting the work tree under /boot, and changes to the guard settings ask first), full deny / ask rules, dialogExpiry of 10 minutes, and a push notification when your attention is needed.
- Existing installs get dialogExpiry added on upgrade (the original file is backed up).
- steward status shows the running version and the Remote Control mode; the steward.conf template gains an auto mode example.
- Fixes: upgrading from claude-unraid kept the old config and dropped --spawn same-dir; the fake root used by tests no longer touches the real service or syslog.

### 2026.10.23
- Renamed to Steward (formerly claude-unraid). Anthropic's trademark guidelines do not allow product names to contain Claude / Claude Code; this plugin is an independent community project, not affiliated with Anthropic or Lime Technology.
- Install migrates from the old version automatically: /boot/claude-code -> /boot/steward (a symlink is kept at the old path), old hooks removed, old plugin file disabled.
- Remote Control now defaults to --spawn same-dir: new sessions opened from the phone run directly in the workspace instead of falling into a worktree copy because /boot is a git repo.
- Command renamed to steward; settings page moved to Settings -> Steward.

### 2026.10.12
- First release: the claude-unraid script packaged as a plugin. Loads automatically at boot, no more editing the go file.
- Settings -> Claude Code: view status, start / stop / restart, sign in and first-time setup.
- Steps that take a while show progress.
</CHANGES>

<!-- 1. Pre-install: remove the previous version's files -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE><![CDATA[
# The plugin manager does not overwrite files that already exist, so first clear the ones the previous version left in RAM
D=/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/steward
rm -f "$D/README.md" "$D/Steward.page" "$D/include/exec.php" "$D/scripts/steward.sh"
rm -rf "$D/skills"
exit 0
]]></INLINE>
</FILE>

<!-- 2. README shown on the Plugins page -->
<FILE Name="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/steward/README.md">
<INLINE><![CDATA[
**Steward**

Runs Claude Code as an always-on service on this Unraid server, reachable from the Claude app or claude.ai/code through Remote Control. Independent of the array and Docker: keeps working while the array is stopped. Data lives on the boot device at /boot/steward.

Steward is an independent, community-maintained project, not affiliated with, endorsed by, or sponsored by Anthropic PBC or Lime Technology, Inc. "Claude" and "Claude Code" are trademarks of Anthropic PBC; "Unraid" is a trademark of Lime Technology, Inc. It downloads the unmodified Claude Code binary from Anthropic's release server, pinned to one version and verified against its published SHA256; installing over the plugin's earlier name, claude-unraid (same author), migrates the data and removes the old plugin; you sign in with your own Claude subscription through Anthropic's own login flow, and usage is billed to your own account.
]]></INLINE>
</FILE>

<!-- 3. Settings page: Settings -> Steward -->
<FILE Name="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/steward/Steward.page">
<INLINE><![CDATA[
Menu="Utilities"
Title="Steward"
Icon="terminal"
Tag="terminal"
Markdown="false"
---
<?PHP
/* Steward's settings page. Independent community project, not affiliated with Anthropic or Lime Technology.
 * This page only displays: every action goes through include/exec.php to /boot/steward/steward.sh. */
?>
<style>
#cu{max-width:110rem}
#cu .cu-bar{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;gap:1rem 1.2rem;margin:0 0 1.6rem}
#cu .cu-bar input[type="button"]{margin:0}
#cu-busy{display:none;white-space:nowrap}
#cu-msg{display:none;margin:0 0 1.6rem;color:var(--brand-orange,#e68a00)}
#cu pre{white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere;margin:0 0 1.6rem;padding:1rem 1.4rem;line-height:2.1rem}
#cu-job{display:none;border-left:3px solid var(--brand-orange,#e68a00)}
#cu-status.cu-stale{opacity:.45}
#cu .cu-note{color:var(--alt-text-color,#808080);line-height:2.3rem}
#cu .cu-note p{margin:0 0 .8rem}
</style>

<div id="cu">
  <div class="cu-bar">
    <input type="button" id="cu-start" value="Start" disabled>
    <input type="button" id="cu-stop" value="Stop" disabled>
    <input type="button" id="cu-restart" value="Restart" disabled>
    <input type="button" id="cu-setup" value="Sign in &amp; first-time setup">
    <span id="cu-busy"><i class="fa fa-circle-o-notch fa-spin fa-fw"></i> <span id="cu-busy-text"></span></span>
  </div>
  <div id="cu-msg"></div>
  <pre id="cu-job"></pre>
  <pre id="cu-status">Loading...</pre>
  <div class="cu-note">
    <p><strong>First time</strong>: click "Sign in &amp; first-time setup". A terminal window opens and walks you through signing in to claude.ai and confirming Remote Control. If the browser blocks the window, allow pop-ups for this site, or run <code>steward setup</code> in the Unraid terminal instead.</p>
    <p>The service is independent of the array and Docker: it keeps working while the array is stopped or the disks are spun down. Login credentials, conversation history and binary backups live on the boot device at <code>/boot/steward</code>; the rules and permissions for Claude are <code>CLAUDE.md</code> and <code>settings.json</code> in <code>/boot/steward/home/</code>.</p>
    <p>Everything else is in the terminal: <code>steward status</code>, <code>steward logs</code>, <code>steward update</code>.</p>
    <p>Steward is an independent, community-maintained project, not affiliated with, endorsed by, or sponsored by Anthropic PBC or Lime Technology, Inc. "Claude" and "Claude Code" are trademarks of Anthropic PBC; "Unraid" is a trademark of Lime Technology, Inc. It runs the unmodified Claude Code binary; you sign in with your own Claude subscription through Anthropic's own login flow.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<script>
$(function() {
  var api = '/plugins/steward/include/exec.php';
  var timer = null, gen = 0, wasBusy = false, label = 'Reading status...';
  var doing = {start: 'Starting...', stop: 'Stopping...', restart: 'Restarting...'};
  var linkRe = /https:\/\/claude\.ai\/[A-Za-z0-9\/_\-?=&%.~+#:@]*/g;

  // Always render as plain text; only claude.ai URLs become links
  function fill(el, text) {
    var last = 0, m, a;
    el.textContent = '';
    linkRe.lastIndex = 0;
    while ((m = linkRe.exec(text)) !== null) {
      el.appendChild(document.createTextNode(text.slice(last, m.index)));
      a = document.createElement('a');
      a.href = m[0];
      a.target = '_blank';
      a.rel = 'noopener noreferrer';
      a.textContent = m[0];
      el.appendChild(a);
      last = m.index + m[0].length;
    }
    el.appendChild(document.createTextNode(text.slice(last)));
  }

  // Backend reply: the first lines are flags like "busy 0", then "--- job" and the job log; status adds a "--- status" section
  function parse(text) {
    var r = {busy: false, service: false, enabled: false, job: '', status: null};
    var lines = String(text).split('\n'), sec = '', job = [], st = [], i, m;
    if (lines.length && lines[lines.length - 1] === '') lines.pop();
    for (i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
      if (lines[i] === '--- job') { sec = 'job'; continue; }
      if (lines[i] === '--- status') { sec = 'status'; continue; }
      if (sec === 'job') job.push(lines[i]);
      else if (sec === 'status') st.push(lines[i]);
      else if ((m = /^(busy|service|enabled) ([01])$/.exec(lines[i]))) r[m[1]] = m[2] === '1';
      else return null;
    }
    if (sec === '') return null;
    r.job = job.join('\n');
    if (sec === 'status') r.status = st.join('\n');
    return r;
  }

  function busy(on, text) {
    if (on) { $('#cu-busy-text').text(text); $('#cu-busy').css('display', 'inline-block'); }
    else $('#cu-busy').hide();
  }

  function note(text) {
    if (text) $('#cu-msg').text(text).show(); else $('#cu-msg').hide();
  }

  function show(r) {
    $('#cu-start').prop('disabled', r.busy || r.service);
    $('#cu-stop').prop('disabled', r.busy || !(r.service || r.enabled));
    $('#cu-restart').prop('disabled', r.busy || !r.service);
    busy(r.busy, label);
    $('#cu-status').toggleClass('cu-stale', r.busy); // While a job runs, the status block below is stale, so dim it
    if (r.job) { fill($('#cu-job')[0], r.job); $('#cu-job').show(); } else $('#cu-job').hide();
    if (r.status !== null) fill($('#cu-status')[0], r.status);
  }

  function later(ms, full) {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(function() { refresh(full); }, ms);
  }

  function broken(text) {
    busy(false);
    $('#cu-status').text(text ? 'Could not read status:\n' + String(text).slice(0, 600) : 'Could not read status (webGUI not responding). Retrying automatically.');
    later(5000, true);
  }

  // Poll progress every second while a job runs; refresh the full status occasionally when idle
  function refresh(full) {
    var g = ++gen;
    $.post(api, {action: full ? 'status' : 'poll'}).done(function(text) {
      if (g !== gen) return;
      var r = parse(text);
      if (!r) { broken(text); return; }
      show(r);
      if (r.busy) { wasBusy = true; later(1000, false); }
      else if (wasBusy) { wasBusy = false; label = 'Working...'; later(0, true); }
      else { label = 'Working...'; later(document.hidden ? 60000 : 15000, true); }
    }).fail(function() {
      if (g === gen) broken('');
    });
  }

  function run(action) {
    label = doing[action];
    note('');
    $('#cu-start,#cu-stop,#cu-restart').prop('disabled', true);
    $('#cu-job').hide().text('');
    $('#cu-status').addClass('cu-stale');
    busy(true, label);
    clearTimeout(timer);
    gen++;
    $.post(api, {action: action}).always(function() {
      wasBusy = true;
      later(300, false);
    });
  }

  $('#cu-start').on('click', function() { run('start'); });
  $('#cu-stop').on('click', function() { run('stop'); });
  $('#cu-restart').on('click', function() { run('restart'); });

  // Open the window right on click (otherwise the browser blocks it as a pop-up), then load it once the backend has the terminal ready
  $('#cu-setup').on('click', function() {
    var manual = 'run steward setup in the Unraid terminal instead', w = null, h = Math.min(screen.availHeight, 800), wd = Math.min(screen.availWidth, 1200);
    note('');
    try {
      w = typeof makeWindow === 'function' ? makeWindow('steward-setup', h, wd) : window.open('', 'steward-setup', 'resizeable=yes,scrollbars=yes,height=' + h + ',width=' + wd);
    } catch (e) { w = null; }
    $.post(api, {action: 'terminal'}).done(function(text) {
      text = $.trim(text);
      if (text !== 'ok') { if (w) w.close(); note(text || 'The terminal did not start; ' + manual + '.'); return; }
      if (!w) { note('The browser blocked the new window. Allow pop-ups for this site and click again, or ' + manual + '.'); return; }
      setTimeout(function() { w.location = '/logterminal/steward/'; w.focus(); }, 200);
    }).fail(function() {
      if (w) w.close();
      note('The webGUI is not responding; ' + manual + '.');
    });
  });

  // Refresh right away when switching back from the setup window
  $(window).on('focus', function() { if (!wasBusy) later(300, true); });

  busy(true, label);
  refresh(true);
});
</script>
]]></INLINE>
</FILE>

<!-- 4. Backend for the settings page -->
<FILE Name="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/steward/include/exec.php">
<INLINE><![CDATA[
<?PHP
/* Backend for the steward settings page.
 * POST only: Unraid validates the CSRF token on every POST first (local_prepend.php), so it is not checked again here.
 * The only runnable actions are the fixed strings in the table below; nothing sent by the page reaches the command line. */
header('Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8');
header('Cache-Control: no-store');
if (($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] ?? '') !== 'POST') {
  http_response_code(405);
  exit("POST only\n");
}
$actions = [
  'status'   => 'status',
  'poll'     => 'poll',
  'start'    => 'run start',
  'stop'     => 'run stop',
  'restart'  => 'run restart',
  'terminal' => 'terminal',
];
$action = $_POST['action'] ?? '';
if (!is_string($action) || !isset($actions[$action])) {
  http_response_code(400);
  exit("Unsupported action\n");
}
$script = '/boot/steward/steward.sh';
if (!is_file($script)) $script = '/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/steward/scripts/steward.sh';
if (!is_file($script)) exit("steward.sh not found; reinstall the plugin\n");
passthru('bash '.escapeshellarg($script).' _gui '.$actions[$action].' 2>&1');
]]></INLINE>
</FILE>

<!-- 5. Main script. The install step copies it to /boot/steward/steward.sh, which is what normally runs -->
<FILE Name="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/steward/scripts/steward.sh" Mode="0755">
<INLINE><![CDATA[
#!/bin/bash
[ -f "$0" ] && grep -q $'\r' "$0" && { t=$(mktemp /tmp/.steward.XXXXXX) && tr -d '\r' <"$0" >"$t" && exec bash "$t" "$@"; } # If the file was saved with Windows line endings, convert it and re-run
#
# Steward - runs Claude Code as a resident service on an Unraid host, operated from your phone or browser via Remote Control.
# Does not depend on the array or on Docker: the service keeps running while the array is stopped.
#
# Copyright (c) 2026 hom-wang (https://github.com/hom-wang)
# Steward is an independent, community-maintained project. It is not affiliated with, endorsed by,
# or sponsored by Anthropic PBC or Lime Technology, Inc. "Claude" and "Claude Code" are trademarks of
# Anthropic PBC; "Unraid" is a trademark of Lime Technology, Inc. This tool downloads the unmodified
# Claude Code binary from Anthropic's release server, pinned to one version and checked against its published SHA256, and runs it as a background service. You sign
# in with your own Claude subscription through Anthropic's own login flow; Steward never sees, stores,
# or forwards your credentials, and all usage is billed to your own account.
#
# Requires: Unraid 7.3 or later with Internal Boot (/boot is ZFS). USB (FAT) boot is not supported.
# Pick one install method (do not install both):
#   Plugin: install steward.plg (which bundles this script) via Plugins -> Install Plugin in the webGUI,
#           then operate it under Settings -> Steward; steward <command> in a terminal works too.
#   Script: bash steward.sh install        then just run steward <command>
# Upgrading from the old name claude-unraid: just install; data is moved automatically from /boot/claude-code to /boot/steward,
#   the old path is kept as a symlink, and the old hooks and old plugin files are removed.
#
# Where things live
#   /boot/steward/           on the boot drive, still there while the array is stopped
#     steward.sh           this script
#     steward.conf         service parameters
#     home/                      Claude Code's config directory: login credentials, settings.json, CLAUDE.md, conversation history
#     workspace/                 working directory for Remote Control
#     cache/                     backup of the Claude Code binary; restored to RAM at boot, no network needed
#   Deliberately not under /boot/config: Unraid Connect's flash backup uploads the whole config/ to the cloud,
#   and login credentials should not go with it. /boot/config only holds hooks with nothing secret in them:
#     one line in stop, plugins/dynamix/steward.cron (watchdog),
#     plus one line in go (script install) or plugins/steward.plg (plugin install).
#   The webGUI's local Flash Backup zips the whole boot drive, which includes this directory; treat that zip as a secret.
#   The binary itself lives in RAM (/root/.local/share/claude). In remote-control mode Claude Code does not update itself,
#   so the watchdog checks once a day after 08:00 and restarts to apply the update only when no session is in use (see auto_update).

set -u
STEWARD_VERSION="2026.10.26"
SPIN_PID="" SPIN_FILE="" SPIN_MSG="" SPIN_T0=0 SPIN_ACTIVE=0 SPIN_OWNER=$$ FANCY=0 TTY_FD=2 HOLD_WINDOW=0
cleanup() { # every exit path ends up here, including Ctrl+C and the terminal being closed
  if [ -n "$SPIN_PID" ]; then # progress display still running: stop it and wipe that line
    kill "$SPIN_PID" 2>/dev/null
    wait "$SPIN_PID" 2>/dev/null
    ((FANCY)) && printf '\r\033[K' >&"$TTY_FD"
  fi
  [ -n "$SPIN_FILE" ] && rm -f "$SPIN_FILE" "$SPIN_FILE.new"
  rm -f "/tmp/.steward-install.$$".* 2>/dev/null
  case "$0" in /tmp/.steward.*) rm -f "$0" ;; esac
  if ((HOLD_WINDOW)) && [ -t 0 ]; then # terminal window opened from the settings page: pause, otherwise the result vanishes as soon as the program exits
    printf '\nYou can close this window now (press any key)' >&"$TTY_FD"
    read -r -n 1 -s _
  fi
  return 0
}
trap cleanup EXIT
trap 'exit 130' INT

P="${STEWARD_TEST_ROOT:-}" # fake root for tests only; empty in production
ROOT="$P/boot/steward"
SELF="$ROOT/steward.sh"
CONF="$ROOT/steward.conf"
CFG_DIR="$ROOT/home"
WORKSPACE="$ROOT/workspace"
CACHE="$ROOT/cache"
ENABLED="$ROOT/enabled" # this file exists = start automatically at boot
GO="$P/boot/config/go"
STOP="$P/boot/config/stop"
CRON="$P/boot/config/plugins/dynamix/steward.cron"
PLUGIN_DIR="$P/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/steward"
BIN_DIR="$P/usr/local/bin"
RUN_DIR="$P/var/run"
LOG="$P/var/log/steward.log"
PIDFILE="$RUN_DIR/steward.pid"
LOCKFILE="$RUN_DIR/steward.lock"
DOWN_FLAG="$RUN_DIR/steward.shutdown" # shutting down: do not bring the service back up
ARRAY_MNT="$P/mnt"
TAG="STEWARD_SVC" # every process under the service carries this env var; that is how we identify them
[ -n "$P" ] && TAG="STEWARD_SVC_TEST" # fake-root tests: use a different tag so tagged_pids / kill_tagged never touch the real service
MARK="# steward"
PLG="$P/boot/config/plugins/steward.plg"   # this file exists = installed via the plugin
INSTALL_LOG="$RUN_DIR/steward.install.log" # most recent output of the Claude Code download or update
JOBLOG="$RUN_DIR/steward.job.log"          # jobs triggered by settings-page buttons write their output here for the web page to read
JOBLOCK="$RUN_DIR/steward.job.lock"
PLUGIN=0
[ -f "$PLG" ] && PLUGIN=1

export HOME="$P/root"
export CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR="$CFG_DIR"
export PATH="$BIN_DIR:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin:$HOME/.local/bin"
case "${LC_ALL:-${LANG:-}}" in *[Uu][Tt][Ff]*) ;; *) export LANG=en_US.UTF-8 ;; esac # the boot script's environment has no locale; en_US.UTF-8 is the only UTF-8 locale Unraid ships (C.UTF-8 does not exist and makes perl/virsh warn)
NATIVE_BIN="$HOME/.local/bin/claude"
VERSIONS="$HOME/.local/share/claude/versions"
# Where Claude Code releases come from, and the one version a fresh install downloads. The binary is only installed if its
# SHA256 matches the pinned value below (taken from Anthropic's published manifest for that version). Both values are
# updated with each Steward release; later updates go through Claude Code's own updater and are checked against the manifest.
CLAUDE_RELEASES="https://downloads.claude.ai/claude-code-releases"
CLAUDE_PLATFORM="linux-x64"
CLAUDE_PIN_VERSION="2.1.308"
CLAUDE_PIN_SHA256="356733f185fa988e6bf3aa9a555269fdd167982a2e2a80d3c8a78037bb92ad58"

if [ -n "${STEWARD_TEST_FAST:-}" ]; then TICK=2 BASE=1 HEALTHY=4 NETWAIT=1 BEAT=2; else TICK=600 BASE=5 HEALTHY=300 NETWAIT=10 BEAT=10; fi

# The progress animation is drawn only on an interactive terminal; when output goes to a file or pipe, print one line per step instead.
# Keep a separate copy of the terminal in TTY_FD: if Ctrl+C is pressed while a step's stderr is temporarily redirected to the log, cleanup still has to be able to wipe that line
if [ -t 1 ] && [ -t 2 ] && [ "${TERM:-dumb}" != dumb ]; then
  FANCY=1
  exec {TTY_FD}>&2
fi
SPIN_FRAMES='⠋⠙⠹⠸⠼⠴⠦⠧⠇⠏' OK_MARK='✔' BAD_MARK='✘' PLAIN=0
case "${TERM:-}" in linux | vt[0-9]*) PLAIN=1 ;; esac # local console attached to a screen: its font lacks these glyphs
[ "${#SPIN_FRAMES}" -le 10 ] || PLAIN=1              # when the locale is not UTF-8, that string is counted as 30 bytes
if ((PLAIN)); then SPIN_FRAMES='|/-'"\\" OK_MARK='*' BAD_MARK='x'; fi

RC_ARGS=(--spawn same-dir --capacity 4)
# shellcheck disable=SC1090
[ -f "$CONF" ] && . <(tr -d '\r' <"$CONF")

say() { printf '%s\n' "$*"; }
warn() { printf 'Warning: %s\n' "$*" >&2; }
err() { printf 'Error: %s\n' "$*" >&2; }
die() {
  [ -n "$SPIN_PID" ] && spin_stop clear
  err "$*"
  exit 1
}
log() {
  printf '%s [steward] %s\n' "$(date '+%F %T')" "$*" >>"$LOG" 2>/dev/null
  [ -z "$P" ] && { logger -t steward -- "$*" 2>/dev/null || true; } # fake-root tests do not write to syslog
  return 0
}
strip_ansi() { sed -u -e 's/\x1b\[[0-9;?]*[A-Za-z]//g' -e 's/\x1b\][^\x07\x1b]*\(\x07\|\x1b\\\)//g' -e 's/\r/\n/g'; }

notify_user() { # $1=subject $2=body $3=level (default warning). Uses Unraid's built-in notifications, which forward to the email / Telegram etc. you configured
  local n="$P/usr/local/emhttp/webGui/scripts/notify"
  [ -x "$n" ] && "$n" -e "Steward" -s "$1" -d "$2" -i "${3:-warning}" >/dev/null 2>&1
  log "Notification: $1 - $2"
}

# ───────────────────────── Progress display while waiting ─────────────────────────
# Usage: spin_start "message" -> do the work (spin_msg can change the text along the way) -> spin_stop ok|fail|clear ["final message"]
# The work runs in the foreground and the progress is drawn in the background, so Ctrl+C interrupts the command that is actually running and leaves nothing running in the background.
# With a terminal, draw the animation. Without one (plugin install window, settings-page job log, output redirected to a file) print one line per step,
# and for long steps add a line every 10 seconds so people know it is still running.

spin_extra() { # $1=path(s) to measure (may be empty or several). Prints a note like "  123 MB  45 s"
  local sz=0 secs=$((SECONDS - SPIN_T0)) out=""
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  [ -n "$1" ] && sz=$(du -sm $1 2>/dev/null | awk '{s += $1} END {print s + 0}')
  ((${sz:-0} > 0)) && out="  $sz MB"
  ((secs >= 3)) && out="$out  $secs s"
  printf '%s' "$out"
}

spin_loop() { # background loop. Quits on its own once the shell that started it is gone, so it never keeps scribbling on the terminal
  trap 'kill ${!:-} 2>/dev/null; exit 0' TERM
  local i=0 n=${#SPIN_FRAMES} msg="" path="" line="" cols a
  if ((!FANCY)); then
    while
      sleep "$BEAT" >/dev/null 2>&1 & # sleep gets no output: if it is orphaned, whatever reads this pipe will not hang waiting on it
      wait $!
      [ -e "$SPIN_FILE" ] && [ -d "/proc/$SPIN_OWNER" ]
    do
      { IFS= read -r msg && IFS= read -r path; } 2>/dev/null <"$SPIN_FILE"
      say "  $msg, still running$(spin_extra "$path")"
    done
    return 0
  fi
  sleep 0.4 >/dev/null 2>&1 &
  wait $! # steps that finish in under half a second are not drawn, to avoid a flicker
  while [ -e "$SPIN_FILE" ] && [ -d "/proc/$SPIN_OWNER" ]; do
    if ((i % 10 == 0)); then # text refreshes once a second
      { IFS= read -r msg && IFS= read -r path; } 2>/dev/null <"$SPIN_FILE"
      line="$msg$(spin_extra "$path")"
      cols=$(stty size <&"$TTY_FD" 2>/dev/null | cut -d' ' -f2) # order matters: attach stdin to the terminal first, then discard the error message
      a=${line//[^[:ascii:]]/} # ASCII counts as 1 column, CJK as 2. Truncate past the window width; wrapping would garble the display
      while [ -n "$line" ] && ((${#a} + 2 * (${#line} - ${#a}) > ${cols:-80} - 3)); do
        line=${line%?}
        a=${line//[^[:ascii:]]/}
      done
    fi
    printf '\r\033[K\033[36m%s\033[0m %s' "${SPIN_FRAMES:i%n:1}" "$line" >&"$TTY_FD"
    i=$((i + 1))
    sleep 0.1 >/dev/null 2>&1 &
    wait $!
  done
}

spin_start() { # spin_start [-t] "in-progress message". -t is for short query-type waits: leaves no trace when done
  local transient=0
  if [ "${1:-}" = -t ]; then
    transient=1
    shift
  fi
  SPIN_MSG=$1 SPIN_T0=$SECONDS SPIN_ACTIVE=$((1 - transient)) SPIN_OWNER=$BASHPID SPIN_FILE="" SPIN_PID=""
  if ((!FANCY)); then
    ((transient)) && return 0
    say "... $1"
  fi
  SPIN_FILE=$(mktemp /tmp/.steward-spin.XXXXXX 2>/dev/null) || SPIN_FILE=""
  [ -n "$SPIN_FILE" ] || return 0
  printf '%s\n' "$1" >"$SPIN_FILE"
  spin_loop &
  SPIN_PID=$!
}

spin_msg() { # spin_msg "new message" [path]. With a path, also show how many MB it currently occupies, so you can tell whether a download is moving
  ((SPIN_ACTIVE)) || return 0
  ((FANCY)) || say "... $1"
  [ -n "$SPIN_FILE" ] && printf '%s\n%s\n' "$1" "${2:-}" >"$SPIN_FILE.new" 2>/dev/null && mv -f "$SPIN_FILE.new" "$SPIN_FILE" 2>/dev/null
  return 0
}

spin_stop() { # spin_stop ok|fail|clear ["final message"]
  local how=${1:-ok} msg=${2:-$SPIN_MSG} secs=$((SECONDS - SPIN_T0)) took=""
  if [ -n "$SPIN_PID" ]; then
    kill "$SPIN_PID" 2>/dev/null
    wait "$SPIN_PID" 2>/dev/null
    ((FANCY)) && printf '\r\033[K' >&"$TTY_FD"
  fi
  [ -n "$SPIN_FILE" ] && rm -f "$SPIN_FILE" "$SPIN_FILE.new"
  SPIN_PID="" SPIN_FILE="" SPIN_ACTIVE=0
  ((secs >= 3)) && took=" ($secs s)"
  case "$how" in
  ok) if ((FANCY)); then printf '\033[32m%s\033[0m %s%s\n' "$OK_MARK" "$msg" "$took"; else say "$OK_MARK $msg$took"; fi ;;
  fail) if ((FANCY)); then printf '\033[31m%s\033[0m %s\n' "$BAD_MARK" "$msg" >&2; else printf '%s %s\n' "$BAD_MARK" "$msg" >&2; fi ;;
  esac
  return 0
}

# ───────────────────────── Process management ─────────────────────────

tagged_pids() { # every process started by the service. Identified by the env var, so it does not matter if a process calls setsid or gets adopted by init
  local f
  grep -lszxF "$TAG=1" /proc/[0-9]*/environ 2>/dev/null | while IFS= read -r f; do
    f=${f#/proc/}
    f=${f%/environ}
    [ "$f" = "$$" ] || echo "$f"
  done
}

kill_tagged() { # $1=seconds to wait. TERM first, KILL only on timeout
  local grace=$1 pids i
  pids=$(tagged_pids)
  [ -z "$pids" ] && return 0
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  kill -TERM $pids 2>/dev/null
  for ((i = 0; i < grace * 10; i++)); do
    [ -z "$(tagged_pids)" ] && return 0
    sleep 0.1
  done
  pids=$(tagged_pids)
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  [ -n "$pids" ] && kill -KILL $pids 2>/dev/null
  return 0
}

sup_pid() { # print the running supervisor's pid; return 1 if it is not running
  local pid
  pid=$(cat "$PIDFILE" 2>/dev/null) || return 1
  [[ $pid =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] && [ -r "/proc/$pid/cmdline" ] || return 1
  tr '\0' ' ' 2>/dev/null <"/proc/$pid/cmdline" | grep -q -- ' _supervise' || return 1
  echo "$pid"
}

close_fds() { # call in the subshell that is about to exec into a background process: do not inherit the caller's open files other than 0, 1, 2 (and the one given in $1)
  local fd keep=${1:-}
  for fd in /proc/"$BASHPID"/fd/*; do
    fd=${fd##*/}
    [[ $fd =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || continue
    ((fd <= 2 || fd == 255)) && continue
    [ "$fd" = "$keep" ] && continue
    eval "exec $fd>&-"
  done 2>/dev/null
  return 0
}

launch() {
  [ -n "$P" ] && { log "Test mode (STEWARD_TEST_ROOT): not starting the service"; return 0; } # never start real processes inside the fake root
  sup_pid >/dev/null && return 0
  [ -f "$SELF" ] || return 1
  kill_tagged 15 # supervisor is gone but leftover processes remain (e.g. it was killed unexpectedly): clear them first, so two remote-controls do not fight over the same directory
  # cd /: the supervisor must not inherit the caller's working directory; if that is under /mnt it would block the array.
  # exec: leave no intermediate shell holding the caller's terminal
  (
    cd / || exit 1
    close_fds # when started from the web page the caller is php-fpm; its connection must not be held by the resident process
    exec env "$TAG=1" setsid bash "$SELF" _supervise </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1
  ) &
  local i
  for ((i = 0; i < 50; i++)); do
    sup_pid >/dev/null && return 0
    sleep 0.1
  done
  return 1
}

rotate_log() { # claude writes the log in append mode, so it can simply be truncated; /var/log is in RAM and must not be allowed to grow
  local sz
  sz=$(stat -c %s "$LOG" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
  if ((sz > 2097152)); then
    cp -f "$LOG" "$LOG.1" 2>/dev/null
    : >"$LOG"
  fi
}

fail_hint() {
  local last
  last=$(tail -n 40 "$LOG" 2>/dev/null | strip_ansi | grep -v '\[steward\]' |
    grep -a -i -E 'error|not trusted|logged in|login|subscription|token|enable remote control' | tail -n1 | cut -c1-200)
  case "$last" in
  "") echo "Run steward status to see why" ;;
  *ogin* | *"logged in"* | *subscription* | *token* | *"not trusted"* | *"nable Remote Control"*)
    echo "$last / Run in the Unraid terminal: steward setup" ;;
  *) echo "$last / The service keeps retrying; run steward logs for details" ;;
  esac
}

supervise() { # resident loop: restart when claude remote-control exits, backing off gradually on repeated failures
  cd "$ROOT" 2>/dev/null || cd /
  exec 9>"$LOCKFILE"
  flock -n 9 || exit 0
  echo "$$" >"$PIDFILE"
  log "Service started (supervisor pid $$)"
  # child processes always close fd 9, otherwise they keep holding the lock after the supervisor dies and a new one cannot start
  (while sleep "$TICK"; do
    rotate_log
    cache_binary
  done) 9>&- &
  local fails=0 notified=0 t0 ran rc delay
  while [ -f "$ENABLED" ] && [ ! -f "$DOWN_FLAG" ]; do
    if ! cd "$WORKSPACE" 2>/dev/null; then
      log "Cannot enter $WORKSPACE, retrying in 60 s"
      sleep 60 9>&-
      continue
    fi
    if ! claude_ok && ! ensure_binary 9>&-; then
      log "Claude Code is unusable, retrying in 60 s"
      sleep 60 9>&-
      continue
    fi
    rotate_log
    t0=$SECONDS
    "$NATIVE_BIN" remote-control "${RC_ARGS[@]}" </dev/null >>"$LOG" 2>&1 9>&-
    rc=$?
    ran=$((SECONDS - t0))
    [ -f "$ENABLED" ] && [ ! -f "$DOWN_FLAG" ] || break
    if ((ran >= HEALTHY)); then
      fails=0
      notified=0
    else
      fails=$((fails + 1))
    fi
    delay=$((BASE << (fails > 6 ? 6 : (fails > 0 ? fails - 1 : 0)))) # 5,10,20,40,80,160 s...
    ((delay > 300)) && delay=300
    log "remote-control exited (exit $rc, ran $ran s), restarting in $delay s"
    if ((fails >= 6 && notified == 0)); then
      notified=1
      notify_user "Remote Control keeps failing to start" "$(fail_hint)"
    fi
    sleep "$delay" 9>&-
  done
  log "Service stopped"
  rm -f "$PIDFILE"
}

on_array_stop() { # the array is about to stop: if a process under the service is stuck in /mnt (working directory or open file), end it, otherwise the array cannot unmount
  local pid victims=""
  for pid in $(tagged_pids); do
    if readlink "/proc/$pid/cwd" "/proc/$pid/exe" /proc/"$pid"/fd/* 2>/dev/null | grep -q "^$ARRAY_MNT/"; then
      victims="$victims $pid"
    fi
  done
  [ -z "$victims" ] && return 0
  log "Array stopping, ending processes holding $ARRAY_MNT: $victims"
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  kill -TERM $victims 2>/dev/null
  local i left
  for ((i = 0; i < 10; i++)); do # emhttpd waits for this script to finish, so wait at most 2 seconds
    left=""
    for pid in $victims; do
      grep -qszxF "$TAG=1" "/proc/$pid/environ" 2>/dev/null && left="$left $pid"
    done
    [ -z "$left" ] && return 0
    sleep 0.2
  done
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  kill -KILL $left 2>/dev/null
  return 0
}

# ───────────────────────── Claude Code binary ─────────────────────────

claude_ok() { [ -x "$NATIVE_BIN" ] && "$NATIVE_BIN" --version >/dev/null 2>&1; }
claude_ver() { "$NATIVE_BIN" --version 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $1; exit}'; }

file_sha256() { sha256sum "$1" 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $1; exit}'; }
manifest_sha256() { # $1=version. The SHA256 Anthropic publishes for this platform and version, or nothing if it cannot be fetched
  curl -fsS --max-time 20 "$CLAUDE_RELEASES/$1/manifest.json" 2>/dev/null |
    jq -r --arg p "$CLAUDE_PLATFORM" '.platforms[$p].checksum // empty' 2>/dev/null | grep -E '^[0-9a-f]{64}$'
}
other_version() { # $1=version to exclude. The newest other installed version, or nothing
  find "$VERSIONS" -maxdepth 1 -type f -name '[0-9]*' ! -name "$1" ! -name '.*' 2>/dev/null | sed 's|.*/||' | sort -V | tail -n1
}

verify_installed() { # $1=version that Claude Code's own updater just installed. Compare it with the published checksum; on mismatch remove it and point the launcher back
  local f="$VERSIONS/$1" want got prev
  want=$(manifest_sha256 "$1")
  got=$(file_sha256 "$f")
  if [ -n "$want" ] && [ "$got" = "$want" ]; then
    log "Claude Code $1 matches the published SHA256"
    return 0
  fi
  if [ -z "$want" ]; then
    log "Claude Code $1: could not fetch the published SHA256, so it is not kept; retrying later"
  else
    log "Claude Code $1 does NOT match the published SHA256 (expected $want, got $got); removed"
    notify_user "Claude Code $1 failed verification" "The downloaded binary did not match the checksum Anthropic publishes for this version and was removed. The previous version stays in use. See $LOG"
  fi
  rm -f "$f"
  prev=$(other_version "$1")
  if [ -n "$prev" ]; then ln -sfn "$VERSIONS/$prev" "$NATIVE_BIN"; else rm -f "$NATIVE_BIN"; fi
  return 1
}

cache_binary() { # back up the version currently in use to the boot drive, with its SHA256; after an update this follows along
  local cur ver
  cur=$(readlink -f "$NATIVE_BIN" 2>/dev/null) || return 0
  ver=${cur##*/}
  [[ $ver =~ ^[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+ ]] || return 0
  [ -f "$CACHE/claude-$ver" ] && return 0
  [ "$("$cur" --version 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $1; exit}')" = "$ver" ] || return 0 # do not keep a half-downloaded file
  spin_msg "Backing up Claude Code $ver to the boot drive"
  mkdir -p "$CACHE"
  if cp -f "$cur" "$CACHE/.tmp.$$" && mv -f "$CACHE/.tmp.$$" "$CACHE/claude-$ver" && file_sha256 "$CACHE/claude-$ver" >"$CACHE/claude-$ver.sha256"; then
    find "$CACHE" -maxdepth 1 -type f -name 'claude-*' ! -name "claude-$ver" ! -name "claude-$ver.sha256" -delete
    log "Backed up Claude Code $ver to the boot drive"
  else
    rm -f "$CACHE/.tmp.$$" "$CACHE/claude-$ver.sha256"
    log "Backing up Claude Code $ver failed (boot drive out of space?)"
  fi
}

restore_binary() { # restore from the boot drive into the native install layout (versions/<version> plus the launcher shortcut). No network needed
  local f ver tmp want
  f=$(find "$CACHE" -maxdepth 1 -type f -name 'claude-*' ! -name '*.sha256' 2>/dev/null | sort -V | tail -n1)
  [ -n "$f" ] || return 1
  spin_msg "Restoring Claude Code from the boot drive"
  ver=${f##*/claude-}
  # the backup must still match the checksum recorded when it was made; the pinned version must match the pinned value
  want=$(cat "$f.sha256" 2>/dev/null)
  [ "$ver" = "$CLAUDE_PIN_VERSION" ] && want=$CLAUDE_PIN_SHA256
  if [ -n "$want" ] && [ "$(file_sha256 "$f")" != "$want" ]; then
    log "The backup of Claude Code $ver on the boot drive does not match its SHA256; discarding it"
    rm -f "$f" "$f.sha256"
    return 1
  fi
  tmp="$HOME/.local/share/claude/.restore.$$"
  mkdir -p "$VERSIONS" "${NATIVE_BIN%/*}"
  if ! { cp -f "$f" "$tmp" && chmod 755 "$tmp" && mv -f "$tmp" "$VERSIONS/$ver"; }; then
    rm -f "$tmp"
    return 1
  fi
  ln -sfn "$VERSIONS/$ver" "$NATIVE_BIN"
  [ "$(claude_ver)" = "$ver" ] && return 0
  # the restored file does not run. Only when it is identical to the backup does that mean the backup itself is broken: discard it and fall back to downloading
  cmp -s "$f" "$VERSIONS/$ver" && rm -f "$f" "$f.sha256"
  rm -f "$VERSIONS/$ver" "$NATIVE_BIN"
  return 1
}

download_binary() { # download the pinned Claude Code version straight from Anthropic's release server and install it only if its SHA256 matches the pinned value.
  # Only reached on first install, or when the backup on the boot drive is gone. Returns 75 = another download is in progress
  local rc
  (
    flock -n 8 || exit 75
    local i tmp sum
    : >"$INSTALL_LOG"
    spin_msg "Connecting to downloads.claude.ai"
    for ((i = 0; i < 30; i++)); do # the network may not be up yet early in boot; wait up to 5 minutes
      curl -fsS --max-time 10 -o /dev/null "$CLAUDE_RELEASES/$CLAUDE_PIN_VERSION/manifest.json" >>"$INSTALL_LOG" 2>&1 && break
      ((i == 0)) && spin_msg "Waiting for network, retrying every $NETWAIT s (up to 5 minutes)"
      sleep "$NETWAIT"
    done
    ((i < 30)) || { echo "downloads.claude.ai is not reachable" >>"$INSTALL_LOG"; exit 1; }
    mkdir -p "$VERSIONS" "${NATIVE_BIN%/*}" || exit 1
    find "$VERSIONS" -maxdepth 1 -type f -size 0 -delete 2>/dev/null # an interrupted previous run may have left an empty file
    tmp="$VERSIONS/.download.$$"
    spin_msg "Downloading Claude Code $CLAUDE_PIN_VERSION" "$tmp"
    if ! curl -fsSL --max-time 1800 --speed-limit 1024 --speed-time 60 -o "$tmp" "$CLAUDE_RELEASES/$CLAUDE_PIN_VERSION/$CLAUDE_PLATFORM/claude" >>"$INSTALL_LOG" 2>&1; then
      echo "Downloading Claude Code $CLAUDE_PIN_VERSION failed" >>"$INSTALL_LOG"
      rm -f "$tmp"
      exit 1
    fi
    sum=$(file_sha256 "$tmp")
    if [ "$sum" != "$CLAUDE_PIN_SHA256" ]; then
      echo "Claude Code $CLAUDE_PIN_VERSION: SHA256 mismatch, not installed (expected $CLAUDE_PIN_SHA256, got $sum)" >>"$INSTALL_LOG"
      rm -f "$tmp"
      exit 1
    fi
    echo "Claude Code $CLAUDE_PIN_VERSION downloaded, SHA256 verified" >>"$INSTALL_LOG"
    if ! { chmod 755 "$tmp" && mv -f "$tmp" "$VERSIONS/$CLAUDE_PIN_VERSION" && ln -sfn "$VERSIONS/$CLAUDE_PIN_VERSION" "$NATIVE_BIN"; }; then
      rm -f "$tmp"
      exit 1
    fi
    exit 0
  ) 8>"$RUN_DIR/steward.download.lock"
  rc=$?
  ((rc == 0)) || return "$rc"
  cache_binary
  claude_ok
}

ensure_binary() {
  claude_ok && return 0
  if restore_binary; then
    log "Restored Claude Code $(claude_ver) from the boot drive"
    return 0
  fi
  log "No usable backup on the boot drive, downloading Claude Code $CLAUDE_PIN_VERSION instead"
  download_binary
}

need_binary() { # for interactive commands: make sure Claude Code is usable, showing progress if there is a wait; on failure print what the installer said
  claude_ok && return 0
  local rc
  spin_start "Preparing the Claude Code binary"
  ensure_binary 2>>"$INSTALL_LOG"
  rc=$?
  if ((rc == 0)); then
    spin_stop ok "Claude Code $(claude_ver) is ready"
    return 0
  fi
  if ((rc == 75)); then
    spin_stop fail "Another download is in progress, try again later"
  else
    spin_stop fail "Claude Code is not ready"
    if [ -s "$INSTALL_LOG" ]; then strip_ansi <"$INSTALL_LOG" | grep -v '^[[:space:]]*$' | uniq | tail -n 15 >&2; fi
  fi
  return 1
}

# ───────────────────────── Hooks and environment ─────────────────────────

write_exec() { # $1=path, content from stdin
  cat >"$1" && chmod 755 "$1"
}

ensure_runtime() { # all of these live in RAM and are rebuilt at every boot
  mkdir -p "$BIN_DIR" "$PLUGIN_DIR/event" "$RUN_DIR" "${LOG%/*}" "${CRON%/*}"
  chmod 700 "$ROOT" 2>/dev/null

  # claude in any shell uses the config and login on the boot drive
  write_exec "$BIN_DIR/claude" <<EOF
#!/bin/bash
# generated by steward, rebuilt on reboot
export HOME="$HOME" CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR="$CFG_DIR"
case ":\$PATH:" in *":$HOME/.local/bin:"*) ;; *) PATH="\$PATH:$HOME/.local/bin" ;; esac
[ -x "$NATIVE_BIN" ] || { echo "Claude Code is not ready yet, run steward status" >&2; exit 127; }
exec "$NATIVE_BIN" "\$@"
EOF
  write_exec "$BIN_DIR/steward" <<EOF
#!/bin/bash
exec bash "$SELF" "\$@"
EOF

  # array stop events: emhttpd runs /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/*/event/<event name>
  local ev
  for ev in stopping unmounting_disks; do
    write_exec "$PLUGIN_DIR/event/$ev" <<EOF
#!/bin/bash
[ -f "$SELF" ] && exec bash "$SELF" _event "\$1"
exit 0
EOF
  done

  # watchdog: update_cron picks up /boot/config/plugins/dynamix/*.cron
  printf '%s\n' "# steward watchdog" "*/5 * * * * bash $SELF watchdog >/dev/null 2>&1" >"$CRON.new"
  if cmp -s "$CRON.new" "$CRON"; then rm -f "$CRON.new"; else mv -f "$CRON.new" "$CRON"; fi
  [ -e "$P/usr/local/sbin/update_cron" ] && bash "$P/usr/local/sbin/update_cron" >/dev/null 2>&1
  return 0
}

add_hook() { # $1=file $2=command $3=create means the file may be created if it does not exist
  local f=$1 line="$2  $MARK"
  if [ -f "$f" ]; then
    [ "$(grep -cF -- "$MARK" "$f")" = 1 ] && grep -qxF -- "$line" "$f" && return 0 # already exactly this line: do not rewrite the boot drive
    [ -e "$f.before-steward" ] || cp -p "$f" "$f.before-steward"
    sed -i "\|$MARK|d" "$f"
    [ -z "$(tail -c1 "$f")" ] || echo >>"$f"
  elif [ "${3:-}" = create ]; then
    printf '#!/bin/bash\n' >"$f"
  else
    return 1
  fi
  printf '%s\n' "$line" >>"$f"
}

boot_shared_over_smb() {
  command -v testparm >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
  testparm -s 2>/dev/null | grep -Eq '^[[:space:]]*path[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*/boot/?[[:space:]]*$'
}

write_default() { # $1=path, content from stdin. If the file already exists leave it alone, preserving your changes
  if [ -e "$1" ]; then cat >/dev/null; else cat >"$1"; fi
}

write_defaults() {
  write_default "$CONF" <<'EOF'
# Steward service options. After editing, run: steward restart
#
# Arguments passed to claude remote-control.
# --spawn same-dir: new sessions opened from the phone run directly in the workspace. Without it, if /boot is a git repo
#   (it is for anyone using Unraid Connect flash backup), Claude Code switches to worktree mode and sessions run in a copy.
# --capacity is the number of sessions that can be open at once; each session is a separate process and uses memory.
RC_ARGS=(--spawn same-dir --capacity 4)

# Example: auto mode -- a classifier lets low-risk actions through and asks only for risky ones; the deny / ask rules in settings.json and bash-guard still apply.
#   If the plan does not support it, Claude Code falls back to asking for each action and the conversation shows "auto mode is unavailable".
# RC_ARGS=(--spawn same-dir --capacity 4 --permission-mode auto)
# Example: auto-accept file edits only, still ask for every command
# RC_ARGS=(--spawn same-dir --capacity 4 --permission-mode acceptEdits)
EOF

  # Second line of defense: settings.json rules only match the start of a command; this hook checks the whole command (full paths, sh -c, ; chains and pipelines are all visible)
  mkdir -p "$ROOT/hooks"
  write_default "$ROOT/hooks/bash-guard.sh" <<'HOOK'
#!/bin/bash
# Steward's PreToolUse hook (matcher: Bash). Reads the tool call JSON from stdin and checks the WHOLE command string,
# so when the prefix-only rules in settings.json are bypassed with full paths, sh -c, ; chains, pipelines or xargs, this still catches it.
#   deny: disk wipe / format tools, reading password hashes / keys / tokens
#   ask : destructive or host-level commands, writing /boot/config from the shell, git rewriting the work tree of the /boot repo,
#         modifying this hook or settings.json
# Everything else is handed back to the normal permission rules (in auto mode the classifier decides).
# Known limitation: text matching only. A command that merely "mentions" a keyword is caught too (e.g. echo rm); commands inside
# a script file being run (bash x.sh, . x.sh) do not pass through here again. This is a deliberate trade-off.

cmd=$(jq -r '.tool_input.command // empty' 2>/dev/null) || exit 0
[[ -z "$cmd" ]] && exit 0

emit() { # $1=deny|ask $2=reason
  jq -cn --arg d "$1" --arg r "$2" \
    '{hookSpecificOutput:{hookEventName:"PreToolUse",permissionDecision:$d,permissionDecisionReason:$r}}'
  exit 0
}

# ---------- DENY: never allowed, anywhere in the command ----------
if [[ "$cmd" =~ (^|[^[:alnum:]_.-])(mkfs(\.[[:alnum:]]+)?|wipefs|sgdisk|blkdiscard|shred)([^[:alnum:]_-]|$) ]]; then
  emit deny "bash-guard: disk wipe/format tools are always forbidden (use the webGUI)"
fi
if [[ "$cmd" =~ zpool[[:space:]]+(destroy|labelclear) ]]; then
  emit deny "bash-guard: zpool destroy/labelclear is always forbidden"
fi
# Paths of password hashes / keys / tokens. Prefix match, so backups like /etc/shadow- and globs like /etc/shad* are caught too
secret_paths='/etc/g?shad|/boot/config/shad|smbpasswd|secrets\.tdb|/boot/config/ssh|/boot/config/ssl|/boot/config/wireguard|/etc/wireguard|/root/\.ssh|/root/keyfile|\.credentials|unraid-api\.key|/var/lib/samba/private'
if [[ "$cmd" =~ ($secret_paths) ]]; then
  emit deny "bash-guard: command touches a password hash/key/token path; reading or printing it is forbidden"
fi

# ---------- ASK: always ask the user first ----------
# Command position: at the start, or after ; & | ( ` quotes or whitespace (catches sh -c "...", pipelines, chains, timeout/nice/env prefixes), optionally with sudo or a full path
cmdpos='(^|[;&|(`'"'"'"]|[[:space:]])(sudo[[:space:]]+)?(/[[:alnum:]_./-]*/)?'
dangerous='(rm|dd|mdcmd|emcmd|reboot|poweroff|powerdown|shutdown|halt|truncate)'
if [[ "$cmd" =~ ${cmdpos}${dangerous}([[:space:]]|$) ]]; then
  emit ask "bash-guard: contains a delete/overwrite/array-control/shutdown command; needs user confirmation"
fi
if [[ "$cmd" =~ (xargs[[:space:]]+(-[^[:space:]]+[[:space:]]+)*rm([[:space:]]|$)|-exec[[:space:]]+rm([[:space:]]|$)|-delete([[:space:]]|$)) ]]; then
  emit ask "bash-guard: deletes files via xargs/find; needs user confirmation"
fi
if [[ "$cmd" =~ zfs[[:space:]]+(destroy|rollback)|docker[[:space:]]+(rm|rmi|volume[[:space:]]+(rm|prune)|system[[:space:]]+prune|container[[:space:]]+(rm|prune)|image[[:space:]]+prune)|virsh[[:space:]]+(destroy|undefine) ]]; then
  emit ask "bash-guard: removes ZFS/container/VM resources; needs user confirmation"
fi
# The real command hidden inside a string: eval, sh -c (including combined forms like -lc, -ec), base64 decode then execute
if [[ "$cmd" =~ (^|[^[:alnum:]_])eval([[:space:]]|$)|base64[[:space:]]+(-d|--decode)|(^|[^[:alnum:]_])(ba)?sh[[:space:]]+-[a-zA-Z]*c[[:space:]] ]]; then
  emit ask "bash-guard: indirect execution (eval/sh -c/base64 -d); needs user confirmation"
fi

# Writing /boot/config from the shell instead of with the Edit tool. Counts whether the absolute path is used or it first cd's into /boot and then uses a relative config/... path
write_verbs='(^|[^[:alnum:]_])(cp|mv|tee|install|rsync|ln|touch|mkdir|chmod|chown|chattr|patch|dd|truncate|fromdos|todos|dos2unix|unix2dos)([[:space:]]|$)'
inplace_edit='(^|[^[:alnum:]_])(sed[[:space:]]+(-[^[:space:]]*i|--in-place)|perl[[:space:]]+-[^[:space:]]*[ip])'
cd_boot='(^|[;&|[:space:]])cd[[:space:]]+/boot/?([[:space:]]*(&&|;|\|)|[[:space:]]*$)'
targets_boot_config=0
[[ "$cmd" =~ /boot/config ]] && targets_boot_config=1
[[ "$cmd" =~ $cd_boot && "$cmd" =~ (^|[[:space:]>])config/ ]] && targets_boot_config=1
if ((targets_boot_config)); then
  if [[ "$cmd" =~ $write_verbs ]] || [[ "$cmd" =~ $inplace_edit ]] ||
    [[ "$cmd" =~ \>[[:space:]]*(/boot/)?config/ ]] ||
    [[ "$cmd" =~ (^|[^[:alnum:]_])git[[:space:]]+(reset|checkout|restore|clean|stash|rm|mv)([[:space:]]|$) ]]; then
    emit ask "bash-guard: writes /boot/config from the shell (back up to /boot/steward/backups/ first)"
  fi
fi

# git rewriting the work tree: /boot is the git repo of Unraid Connect flash backup, /boot/config is tracked, and our working directory is inside it.
# reset --hard / checkout / restore / stash roll the config back to the last auto-commit; clean deletes files that were never tracked (e.g. wireguard/).
# Only /boot matters: ask when no repo is given (the default is /boot) or -C / --git-dir / --work-tree points at /boot itself; another repo is not asked about
git_opts='([[:space:]]+(-C[[:space:]]*[^[:space:]]+|--git-dir(=|[[:space:]]+)[^[:space:]]+|--work-tree(=|[[:space:]]+)[^[:space:]]+|-c[[:space:]]+[^[:space:]]+|--[a-z-]+(=[^[:space:]]+)?))*'
if [[ "$cmd" =~ (^|[^[:alnum:]_])git${git_opts}[[:space:]]+(reset|clean|checkout|restore|stash)([[:space:]]|$) ]]; then
  sub=${BASH_REMATCH[$((${#BASH_REMATCH[@]} - 2))]}
  repo=""
  if [[ "$cmd" =~ git[[:space:]]+([^[:space:]]+[[:space:]]+)*(-C[[:space:]]*|--git-dir[=[:space:]]|--work-tree[=[:space:]])([^[:space:]]+) ]]; then
    repo=${BASH_REMATCH[3]%/}
    repo=${repo%/.git}
  fi
  rewrites=0
  case "$sub" in
  reset) [[ "$cmd" =~ --hard ]] && rewrites=1 ;;
  checkout) [[ "$cmd" =~ checkout[[:space:]].*(--|[[:space:]]\.)([[:space:]]|$) || "$cmd" =~ checkout[[:space:]]+\.$ ]] && rewrites=1 ;;
  stash) [[ "$cmd" =~ stash[[:space:]]+(list|show) ]] || rewrites=1 ;;
  clean | restore) rewrites=1 ;;
  esac
  [[ "$cmd" =~ --help ]] && rewrites=0
  if ((rewrites)) && { [ -z "$repo" ] || [ "$repo" = /boot ]; }; then
    emit ask "bash-guard: git $sub rewrites the work tree of /boot (/boot/config is in this repo); needs user confirmation"
  fi
fi

# Modifying this hook or the permission settings (the old path /boot/claude-code is a symlink to /boot/steward; cd'ing in first and using a relative path counts too)
guard_target=0
[[ "$cmd" =~ /boot/(steward|claude-code)/(hooks/|home/settings\.json) ]] && guard_target=1
[[ "$cmd" =~ (^|[;&|[:space:]])cd[[:space:]]+/boot/(steward|claude-code)(/home|/hooks)?/?[[:space:]]*(&&|;|\|) && "$cmd" =~ (hooks/|bash-guard|settings\.json) ]] && guard_target=1
if ((guard_target)); then
  if [[ "$cmd" =~ $write_verbs ]] || [[ "$cmd" =~ $inplace_edit ]] || [[ "$cmd" =~ (^|[^[:alnum:]_])(sed|rm|perl)([[:space:]]|$)|\> ]]; then
    emit ask "bash-guard: modifies the permission rules or the guard script; needs user confirmation"
  fi
fi

exit 0
HOOK
  # The hook is invoked with bash, so it needs no execute bit; permissions of an existing file are left alone too

  # dialogExpiry: how long a permission prompt forwarded to the phone waits before it is cancelled automatically (official default 5m)
  write_default "$CFG_DIR/settings.json" <<'EOF'
{
  "dialogExpiry": "10m",
  "hooks": {
    "PreToolUse": [
      {
        "matcher": "Bash",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "bash /boot/steward/hooks/bash-guard.sh",
            "timeout": 10,
            "onFailure": "block",
            "statusMessage": "bash-guard checking"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "permissions": {
    "disableBypassPermissionsMode": "disable",
    "deny": [
      "Bash(mkfs*)",
      "Bash(/sbin/mkfs*)",
      "Bash(/usr/sbin/mkfs*)",
      "Bash(wipefs *)",
      "Bash(/sbin/wipefs *)",
      "Bash(/usr/sbin/wipefs *)",
      "Bash(sgdisk *)",
      "Bash(blkdiscard *)",
      "Bash(shred *)",
      "Bash(zpool destroy *)",
      "Bash(zpool labelclear *)",
      "Read(//etc/shadow)",
      "Read(//boot/config/shadow)",
      "Read(//boot/config/smbpasswd)",
      "Read(//boot/config/secrets.tdb)",
      "Read(//boot/config/ssh/**)",
      "Read(//boot/config/ssl/**)",
      "Read(//boot/config/wireguard/**)",
      "Read(//etc/wireguard/**)",
      "Read(//root/.ssh/**)",
      "Read(//root/keyfile)",
      "Read(//boot/steward/home/.credentials.json)",
      "Read(//boot/steward/home/unraid-api.key)"
    ],
    "ask": [
      "Bash(rm *)",
      "Bash(/bin/rm *)",
      "Bash(/usr/bin/rm *)",
      "Bash(dd *)",
      "Bash(truncate *)",
      "Bash(mdcmd *)",
      "Bash(emcmd *)",
      "Bash(reboot*)",
      "Bash(poweroff*)",
      "Bash(powerdown*)",
      "Bash(shutdown *)",
      "Bash(halt*)",
      "Bash(sh -c *)",
      "Bash(bash -c *)",
      "Bash(eval *)",
      "Bash(git reset --hard*)",
      "Bash(git clean *)",
      "Bash(git checkout -- *)",
      "Bash(git restore *)",
      "Bash(zfs destroy *)",
      "Bash(zfs rollback *)",
      "Bash(docker rm *)",
      "Bash(docker rmi *)",
      "Bash(docker container rm *)",
      "Bash(docker container prune*)",
      "Bash(docker image prune*)",
      "Bash(docker volume rm *)",
      "Bash(docker volume prune*)",
      "Bash(docker system prune*)",
      "Bash(virsh destroy *)",
      "Bash(virsh undefine *)",
      "Edit(//boot/config/**)",
      "Edit(//boot/steward/hooks/**)",
      "Edit(//boot/steward/home/settings.json)"
    ],
    "allow": [
      "Bash(docker ps *)",
      "Bash(docker logs *)",
      "Bash(docker images *)",
      "Bash(docker stats --no-stream *)",
      "Bash(virsh list *)",
      "Bash(df *)",
      "Bash(free *)",
      "Bash(uptime)",
      "Bash(lsblk *)",
      "Bash(sensors *)",
      "Bash(smartctl -a *)",
      "Bash(smartctl -H *)",
      "Bash(zpool status *)",
      "Bash(zpool list *)",
      "Bash(zfs list *)"
    ]
  }
}
EOF

  # Push notifications: push to the phone when a permission prompt or question is waiting for you ("Push when actions required" in /config).
  # Claude Code maintains this file itself after login, so it is only written on a fresh install when it does not exist yet
  write_default "$CFG_DIR/.claude.json" <<'EOF'
{
  "inputNeededNotifEnabled": true
}
EOF

  write_default "$CFG_DIR/CLAUDE.md" <<'EOF'
# This machine is an Unraid server, and you run directly on the host as root

You are the resident admin assistant of this NAS; the user sends instructions through Remote Control from a phone or browser.
There is no container isolation here: every command acts directly on the host, and lost data cannot be recovered. When unsure, ask first.

## What is special about this machine

- The root filesystem lives in RAM: changes outside `/boot` and `/mnt` are gone after a reboot. Settings that must persist go in `/boot/config`, or are rebuilt on every boot by the startup script `/boot/config/go`.
- `/boot` is the boot drive (Internal Boot, ZFS) and stays available while the array is stopped. Space is limited; do not put large files there.
- `/mnt/user/<share>` is a user share (merged view); `/mnt/diskN` and `/mnt/<pool>` are the underlying disks. Never mix `/mnt/user` and `/mnt/diskN` in the same copy or move command; it corrupts files.
- When the array is stopped, `/mnt/user`, `/mnt/disk*` and every pool disappear, and Docker and VMs stop too. You yourself (`/boot/steward`) are unaffected.
- Check the array state before acting: `grep -E '^(mdState|fsState)=' /var/local/emhttp/var.ini`

## Always follow these rules

1. Never leave the working directory under `/mnt`; always use absolute paths. Any process stuck in `/mnt` prevents the array from unmounting.
2. For the following operations, explain the impact first, get the user's explicit consent, and do one thing at a time: stopping or starting the array, rebooting or shutting down, parity checks, changing disk assignments, deleting containers / VMs / shares, updating Unraid or plugins.
3. Never run New Config, clear or format disks, `zpool destroy`, `mkfs` or `wipefs`. Have the user do those themselves in the webGUI.
4. Before modifying any file under `/boot/config`, back it up to `/boot/steward/backups/<datetime>/`, and tell the user what you changed, how to restore it, and whether a service restart or reboot is needed for it to take effect.
5. Do not read or print password hashes, keys, tokens or WireGuard private keys. Conversation logs are stored in plain text on the boot drive and synced to Anthropic.
6. When a long task finishes, or the user needs to make a decision, send an Unraid notification:
   `/usr/local/emhttp/webGui/scripts/notify -e "Steward" -s "<subject>" -d "<body>" -i normal`

## Conventions

- Docker: containers are managed by templates (`/boot/config/plugins/dockerMan/templates-user/my-*.xml`). Do not create containers meant to stay with a manual `docker run`; the webGUI has no template for them, so they cannot be edited or updated in the UI later.
- Config files live in `/boot/config` (`ident.cfg`, `network.cfg`, `share.cfg`, `disk.cfg`, `docker.cfg`, `shares/*.cfg`, etc.). Editing a file directly does not take effect immediately; use the official UI whenever possible.
- Unraid API (GraphQL, built in since 7.2): docs at https://docs.unraid.net/API/ , local CLI via `unraid-api --help`. When an API key is needed, ask the user first, then create it with `unraid-api apikey --create` with the minimum permissions that suffice; store the key as `/boot/steward/home/unraid-api.key` (mode 600) and do not print it.
- Stop or start the array only through the official UI; no `mdcmd stop`, no manual umount.
- Files you create in a share (`/mnt/user/...`) belong to root by default and SMB users cannot open them: after creating them, run `chown nobody:users` and `chmod ug+rw` on those files. Only touch files you created yourself; do not recursively change permissions on appdata or an entire share.
- Logs: system in `/var/log/syslog`, containers via `docker logs <name>`. Diagnostics bundle via `diagnostics` (output in `/boot/logs`).
- Your own service is managed by `steward` (`status`, `restart`, `update`, `logs`). Do not kill claude processes yourself.
EOF

  merge_settings
}

merge_settings() { # When upgrading an existing install, add default keys that only exist in the new version. Only missing keys are added, user changes are untouched; the original is kept as a backup
  local f="$CFG_DIR/settings.json" tmp
  [ -s "$f" ] || return 0
  jq -e 'has("dialogExpiry")' "$f" >/dev/null 2>&1 && return 0
  tmp=$(mktemp "$f.XXXXXX") || return 0
  if jq '.dialogExpiry = "10m"' "$f" >"$tmp" 2>/dev/null && jq -e . "$tmp" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    cp -p "$f" "$f.before-$STEWARD_VERSION"
    mv -f "$tmp" "$f"
    log "settings.json: added dialogExpiry=10m (original backed up as settings.json.before-$STEWARD_VERSION)"
  else
    rm -f "$tmp"
  fi
}

preflight() {
  [ "$(id -u)" = 0 ] || die "Run as root"
  if [ -z "$P" ]; then mountpoint -q /boot || die "/boot is not mounted"; fi
  case "$(stat -f -c %T "$P/boot" 2>/dev/null)" in
  msdos | vfat | fat* | exfat) die "/boot is a FAT filesystem (USB boot). This script stores its data directly on the boot drive and only works with Internal Boot (ZFS)" ;;
  esac
  [ -e "$P/boot/config/plugins/claude-code.plg" ] && die "Another Claude Code plugin (claude-code.plg) is installed; the two would overwrite each other's /usr/local/bin/claude. Pick one"
  mkdir -p "$ROOT" || die "Cannot create $ROOT"
  # Check for real whether the boot drive preserves permissions and symlinks
  local t="$ROOT/.probe.$$" ok=1
  : >"$t" && chmod 600 "$t" && [ "$(stat -c %a "$t")" = 600 ] && ln -s "$t" "$t.l" 2>/dev/null && ok=0
  rm -f "$t" "$t.l"
  [ "$ok" = 0 ] || die "The filesystem holding $ROOT does not support file permissions or symlinks; login credentials cannot be stored there safely"
  local avail
  avail=$(df --output=avail -BM "$P/boot" 2>/dev/null | tail -n1 | tr -dc '0-9')
  [ -n "$avail" ] && ((avail < 1024)) && warn "Only ${avail}MB left on the boot drive; the Claude Code binary backup needs about 300MB"
  return 0
}

# ───────────────────────── Commands ─────────────────────────

install_self() { # Put the script currently running onto the boot drive. Returns 0 if the content changed; 1 if unchanged, and then nothing is written to the boot drive
  local src
  src=$(readlink -f "${BASH_SOURCE[0]:-}" 2>/dev/null)
  [ -f "$src" ] || die "Save the script to a file before running it"
  [ "$src" = "$(readlink -f "$SELF" 2>/dev/null)" ] && return 1
  cmp -s <(tr -d '\r' <"$src" 2>/dev/null) "$SELF" && return 1 # cmp stops reading at the first difference; tr's complaint is not worth showing
  # Write a temp file first, then rename: the running service still has the old file open, and overwriting in place would make it read a mix of old and new
  if ! { tr -d '\r' <"$src" >"$SELF.new" && mv -f "$SELF.new" "$SELF"; }; then die "Cannot write $SELF"; fi
  return 0
}

stop_hook() { add_hook "$STOP" "[ -f $SELF ] && bash $SELF shutdown >/dev/null 2>&1" create; }
drop_go_hook() { if grep -qsF "$MARK" "$GO"; then sed -i "\|$MARK|d" "$GO"; fi; }

LEGACY_ROOT="$P/boot/claude-code"
LEGACY_TAG="CLAUDE_UNRAID_SVC"
migrate_legacy() { # Upgrade from the old name claude-unraid: move the data, stop the old service, remove the old hooks. Done once, only when the old directory exists and the new one does not
  [ -d "$LEGACY_ROOT" ] && [ ! -L "$LEGACY_ROOT" ] || return 0
  if [ -e "$ROOT" ]; then # preflight already ran mkdir: only an empty directory counts as "new directory does not exist"
    [ -d "$ROOT" ] && [ -z "$(ls -A "$ROOT" 2>/dev/null)" ] && rmdir "$ROOT" || return 0
  fi
  log "Found data from the old claude-unraid version, moving it to $ROOT"
  # Processes of the old service carry CLAUDE_UNRAID_SVC. If the upgrade is triggered from Claude inside the old service, it gets stopped here as well; reconnect from the phone once the new service is up
  local pids=""
  [ -z "$P" ] && pids=$(grep -lszxF "$LEGACY_TAG=1" /proc/[0-9]*/environ 2>/dev/null | sed 's#^/proc/##; s#/environ$##' | grep -vx "$$") # With a fake root for testing, leave real processes alone
  if [ -n "$pids" ]; then
    # shellcheck disable=SC2086
    kill -TERM $pids 2>/dev/null
    sleep 3
    # shellcheck disable=SC2086
    kill -KILL $pids 2>/dev/null
  fi
  mv "$LEGACY_ROOT" "$ROOT" || die "Cannot move $LEGACY_ROOT to $ROOT"
  ln -s steward "$LEGACY_ROOT" # Keep a symlink at the old path: /boot/claude-code written in old settings, memory and notes keeps working
  if [ -f "$ROOT/claude-unraid.conf" ]; then
    mv -f "$ROOT/claude-unraid.conf" "$CONF"
    sed -i 's/claude-unraid/steward/g; 1s/^# [sS]teward .*/# Steward service options. After editing, run: steward restart/' "$CONF" # Also rewrites the legacy header line (line 1) to the current English wording
    # New default --spawn same-dir: only added when the old config does not specify --spawn; a user-set value is left alone
    grep -q -- '--spawn' "$CONF" || sed -i 's/^RC_ARGS=(\(.*\))$/RC_ARGS=(\1 --spawn same-dir)/' "$CONF"
  fi
  rm -f "$ROOT/claude-unraid.sh"
  # Old hooks: cron, command shortcut, runtime files, lines tagged "# claude-unraid" in stop/go
  rm -f "$P/boot/config/plugins/dynamix/claude-unraid.cron" "$BIN_DIR/claude-unraid" "$RUN_DIR"/claude-unraid.*
  local f
  for f in "$STOP" "$GO"; do
    [ -f "$f" ] && grep -qF '# claude-unraid' "$f" || continue
    [ -e "$f.before-steward" ] || cp -p "$f" "$f.before-steward"
    sed -i '\|# claude-unraid|d' "$f"
  done
  rm -rf "$P/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/claude-unraid"
  if [ -f "$P/boot/config/plugins/claude-unraid.plg" ]; then
    # Do not run the old plugin's remove section: it would delete the shared /usr/local/bin/claude too. Moving it to plugins-removed stops it from loading
    mkdir -p "$P/boot/config/plugins-removed"
    mv -f "$P/boot/config/plugins/claude-unraid.plg" "$P/boot/config/plugins-removed/"
    rm -f "$P/var/log/plugins/claude-unraid.plg" # the plugin manager's "installed" marker (RAM); otherwise update_cron keeps looking for the old plugin until reboot
  fi
  [ -e "$P/usr/local/sbin/update_cron" ] && bash "$P/usr/local/sbin/update_cron" >/dev/null 2>&1
  # Paths and names inside the old config files (originals backed up)
  for f in "$CFG_DIR/settings.json" "$CFG_DIR/CLAUDE.md"; do
    [ -f "$f" ] || continue
    cp -p "$f" "$f.before-steward"
    sed -i 's#/boot/claude-code#/boot/steward#g; s#claude-unraid#steward#g' "$f"
  done
  log "Migration done: $LEGACY_ROOT -> $ROOT"
  say "Migrated from the old claude-unraid version to $ROOT (old path /boot/claude-code kept as a symlink, old plugin file moved to plugins-removed)"
}

cmd_install() {
  preflight
  migrate_legacy
  mkdir -p "$CFG_DIR" "$WORKSPACE" "$CACHE" "$RUN_DIR" "${LOG%/*}"
  install_self
  write_defaults
  if ((PLUGIN)); then
    drop_go_hook # Installed as a plugin: loaded by the plugin at boot, the line in go is not needed
  else
    # go must not be created when missing: Unraid would run go instead of starting emhttp and the webGUI would not come up
    add_hook "$GO" "[ -f $SELF ] && bash $SELF boot >/dev/null 2>&1 &" ||
      warn "$GO not found, boot hook not added. Add this line to the go file manually: bash $SELF boot &"
  fi
  stop_hook
  ensure_runtime
  need_binary || die "Check the network and rerun: steward install"
  say "Install complete: Claude Code $(claude_ver), data in $ROOT"
  boot_shared_over_smb && warn "The boot drive is currently shared over SMB, and the login credentials are stored on it. On the Main page, open the boot device and set Export under SMB Security Settings to No"
  if [ -t 0 ]; then
    local a
    read -r -p "Log in and complete the Remote Control setup now? [Y/n] " a
    case "$a" in n | N) ;; *)
      cmd_setup
      return
      ;;
    esac
  fi
  say "Next step: steward setup"
}

auth_method() { timeout 20 "$NATIVE_BIN" auth status 2>/dev/null | sed -n 's/.*"authMethod"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"\([^"]*\)".*/\1/p' | head -n1; }

cmd_setup() {
  [ -t 0 ] || die "setup needs an interactive terminal (Unraid web terminal or SSH)"
  need_binary || die "Check the network and try again, or run steward install to see why"
  if [ "$(auth_method)" = "claude.ai" ]; then
    say "Step 1/2: already logged in to claude.ai"
  else
    say "Step 1/2: log in to claude.ai (Remote Control does not support API keys)"
    say "  Paste the URL shown into a browser to log in, then paste the code back here. The URL is long, so widen the terminal window before copying."
    "$NATIVE_BIN" auth login || die "Login was not completed"
  fi
  # Stop the background service and disable it for now, so the watchdog does not bring it back while you are working
  rm -f "$ENABLED"
  stop_service 30
  say ""
  say "Step 2/2: start Remote Control once in the foreground and answer the one-time questions"
  say "  1. Answer y when asked whether to trust this directory and whether to enable Remote Control"
  say "  2. Once the connection URL appears, open the Code tab in the Claude phone app or claude.ai/code and confirm this machine is listed"
  say "  3. Press Ctrl+C to return here"
  say ""
  trap : INT # Ctrl+C only ends claude, not this script
  cd "$WORKSPACE" || die "Cannot enter $WORKSPACE"
  "$NATIVE_BIN" remote-control "${RC_ARGS[@]}"
  cd / || true
  trap 'exit 130' INT
  say ""
  local a
  read -r -p "Did it connect successfully? Enable the resident service (start at boot)? [Y/n] " a
  case "$a" in n | N)
    say "Not enabled. After fixing the problem, run steward setup again, or just steward start"
    return 0
    ;;
  esac
  cmd_start
}

stop_service() { # $1=max seconds to wait $2=what to say when nothing is running (optional)
  if [ -n "$(tagged_pids)" ]; then
    spin_start "Stopping service"
    kill_tagged "$1"
    spin_stop ok "Service stopped"
  elif [ -n "${2:-}" ]; then
    say "$2"
  fi
  rm -f "$PIDFILE"
}

log_size() { stat -c %s "$LOG" 2>/dev/null || echo 0; }

wait_connected() { # $1=size of the log file before start. Wait until Remote Control has really connected (the connection URL appears in the log), or it has definitely failed
  local off=$1 t0=$SECONDS url seen=0
  spin_start "Waiting for Remote Control to connect"
  while ((SECONDS - t0 < 15)); do
    (($(log_size) < off)) && off=0 # The log was just rotated and emptied
    if tail -c "+$((off + 1))" "$LOG" 2>/dev/null | grep -a -q 'remote-control exited'; then
      spin_stop fail "Remote Control did not connect: $(fail_hint)"
      return 1
    fi
    url=$(tail -c "+$((off + 1))" "$LOG" 2>/dev/null | grep -a -o 'https://claude\.ai/code[^[:space:][:cntrl:]"]*' | tail -n1)
    if [ -n "$url" ]; then
      seen=$((seen + 1)) # Keep watching for another second after the URL appears: an error message may contain a URL too, so make sure the program does not exit right after
      if ((seen >= 8)); then
        spin_stop ok "Remote Control connected"
        say "  $url"
        return 0
      fi
    fi
    sleep 0.1
  done
  spin_stop clear
  say "The service is running in the background, but no connection URL has appeared in the log yet. Check claude.ai/code or the phone app for this machine; see steward logs for details"
}

cmd_start() { # Returns 0=service running and connected (or still connecting) 1=not started or not connected
  if ! need_binary; then
    err "Claude Code is not ready yet. Check the network and try again, or run steward install to see why"
    return 1
  fi
  rm -f "$DOWN_FLAG"
  : >"$ENABLED"
  if sup_pid >/dev/null; then
    say "Service already running (pid $(sup_pid)). Check with: steward status"
    return 0
  fi
  local off
  off=$(log_size)
  spin_start "Starting service"
  if ! launch; then
    spin_stop fail "Service did not start, see steward logs"
    return 1
  fi
  spin_stop ok "Service started (pid $(sup_pid)), will start automatically at boot"
  wait_connected "$off"
}

cmd_stop() {
  rm -f "$ENABLED"
  stop_service 30 "Service was not running"
  say "Start at boot disabled. Run steward start to re-enable it"
}

cmd_restart() {
  stop_service 30
  cmd_start
}

cmd_shutdown() { # Called from /boot/config/stop. Leaves the "start at boot" setting alone, and must not hold up the shutdown
  : >"$DOWN_FLAG"
  log "System shutting down, stopping service"
  kill_tagged 10
  rm -f "$PIDFILE"
}

cmd_boot() { # Runs in the background at boot: called from /boot/config/go for script installs, from the plugin's install step for plugin installs
  ensure_runtime
  log "Boot: runtime rebuilt"
  if ! ensure_binary; then
    notify_user "Claude Code could not start" "Both restore and download failed at boot; run steward status"
    exit 1
  fi
  if [ -f "$ENABLED" ]; then launch || log "Boot: service did not start"; fi
}

cmd_watchdog() { # Called by cron every 5 minutes
  [ -f "$ENABLED" ] && [ ! -f "$DOWN_FLAG" ] || return 0
  if sup_pid >/dev/null; then
    auto_update
    return 0
  fi
  claude_ok || ensure_binary || return 0
  log "watchdog: service is gone, restarting"
  launch
}

running_ver() { # Which version the running remote-control uses (differs from claude_ver after an update, before a restart)
  local p exe
  for p in $(tagged_pids); do
    # Only accept processes whose argv[1] is exactly remote-control and whose executable is under versions/; commands in a session that mention this string do not count
    [ "$(tr '\0' '\n' 2>/dev/null <"/proc/$p/cmdline" | sed -n 2p)" = remote-control ] || continue
    exe=$(readlink "/proc/$p/exe" 2>/dev/null) || continue
    exe=${exe% (deleted)}
    case "$exe" in "$VERSIONS"/*) ;; *) continue ;; esac
    echo "${exe##*/}"
    return 0
  done
  return 1
}

sessions_busy() { # If a session wrote to its conversation log in the last 15 minutes, treat it as in use
  find "$CFG_DIR/projects" -name '*.jsonl' -mmin -15 2>/dev/null | grep -q .
}

# In remote-control mode Claude Code does not update itself, so the watchdog does it:
# once a day after 08:00 it checks the stable channel; a new version is installed and backed up to the boot drive, and the service is restarted to apply it only when no session is in use, otherwise it retries an hour later
AU_DONE="$RUN_DIR/steward.autoupdate.done" # Contains the date that has already been handled today
AU_NEXT="$RUN_DIR/steward.autoupdate.next" # Contains the earliest time to try again (epoch seconds)
auto_update() {
  local today now next cur run latest rc
  today=$(date +%F) now=$(date +%s)
  (($(date +%-H) >= 8)) || return 0
  [ "$(cat "$AU_DONE" 2>/dev/null)" = "$today" ] && return 0
  next=$(cat "$AU_NEXT" 2>/dev/null)
  [[ $next =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || next=0
  ((now >= next)) || return 0
  echo $((now + 3600)) >"$AU_NEXT"
  claude_ok || return 0
  run=$(running_ver) || return 0 # remote-control is retrying its start or similar; try next time
  cur=$(claude_ver)
  if [ "$run" = "$cur" ]; then # No version waiting to be applied: check for a new one
    latest=$(curl -fsS --max-time 10 https://downloads.claude.ai/claude-code-releases/stable 2>/dev/null) || return 0
    [[ $latest =~ ^[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$ ]] || return 0
    if [ "$(printf '%s\n' "$latest" "$cur" | sort -V | tail -n1)" = "$cur" ]; then # Already up to date (or newer than stable)
      echo "$today" >"$AU_DONE"
      return 0
    fi
    log "Auto-update: installing Claude Code $latest (current $cur)"
    (
      flock -n 8 || exit 75
      timeout 600 "$NATIVE_BIN" install "$latest" </dev/null >"$INSTALL_LOG" 2>&1 # Even if it hangs, it must not hold the download lock forever
    ) 8>"$RUN_DIR/steward.download.lock"
    rc=$?
    if ((rc == 75)); then
      log "Auto-update: another download is in progress, retrying in an hour"
      return 0
    fi
    if [ "$(claude_ver)" != "$latest" ]; then
      log "Auto-update: $latest did not install properly (exit code $rc), retrying in an hour (output in $INSTALL_LOG)"
      return 0
    fi
    verify_installed "$latest" || return 0 # the updater's download is checked against the published SHA256 before it is kept
    cache_binary
    cur=$latest
  fi
  if sessions_busy; then
    log "Auto-update: $cur is installed, but a session is in use; retrying in an hour"
    return 0
  fi
  log "Auto-update: restarting service to apply $cur (was $run)"
  if ! cmd_restart >/dev/null 2>&1 || [ "$(running_ver)" != "$cur" ]; then
    log "Auto-update: service did not come back properly after the restart, retrying in an hour (the watchdog brings it up if it is gone)"
    return 0
  fi
  echo "$today" >"$AU_DONE"
  notify_user "Claude Code updated to $cur" "Was $run. The service was restarted while no session was in use; conversations on the phone can continue" normal
}

cmd_update() {
  claude_ok || die "Claude Code not found, run first: steward install"
  local before after ver
  before=$(readlink -f "$NATIVE_BIN")
  "$NATIVE_BIN" update # It shows its own progress
  after=$(readlink -f "$NATIVE_BIN")
  ver=${after##*/}
  if [ "$before" != "$after" ]; then
    spin_start "Checking Claude Code $ver against the published SHA256"
    if verify_installed "$ver"; then
      spin_stop ok "Claude Code $ver matches the published SHA256"
    else
      spin_stop fail "Claude Code $ver did not verify and was removed; the previous version stays in use"
      return 1
    fi
  fi
  if [ ! -f "$CACHE/claude-$ver" ]; then
    spin_start "Backing up Claude Code $ver to the boot drive"
    cache_binary
    if [ -f "$CACHE/claude-$ver" ]; then
      spin_stop ok "Backed up Claude Code $ver to the boot drive"
    else
      spin_stop fail "Could not back up to the boot drive; it will be downloaded again at next boot"
    fi
  fi
  if [ "$before" != "$after" ] && sup_pid >/dev/null; then
    say "Version updated, restarting service to apply"
    cmd_restart
  fi
}

rc_opt() { # $1=option name $2=default when unset. Extract that option's value from RC_ARGS
  local i
  for ((i = 0; i < ${#RC_ARGS[@]}; i++)); do
    [ "${RC_ARGS[i]}" = "$1" ] && { echo "${RC_ARGS[i + 1]:-$2}"; return 0; }
  done
  echo "$2"
}

cmd_status() {
  local pid v r m="" url arr smb=0
  if pid=$(sup_pid); then
    say "Service:    running (pid $pid, up $(ps -o etime= -p "$pid" 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' '))"
  elif [ -f "$ENABLED" ]; then
    say "Service:    enabled but not running (the watchdog brings it up within 5 minutes, or run steward start)"
  else
    say "Service:    not enabled (steward setup or steward start)"
  fi
  say "Mode:       permission-mode $(rc_opt --permission-mode default)  spawn $(rc_opt --spawn worktree)  capacity $(rc_opt --capacity 1)"
  spin_start -t "Checking Claude Code and login status"
  v=$(claude_ver)
  [ -n "$v" ] && m=$(auth_method)
  boot_shared_over_smb && smb=1
  spin_stop clear
  if [ -n "$v" ]; then
    say "Version:    $v  boot drive backups: $(find "$CACHE" -maxdepth 1 -name 'claude-*' -printf '%f ' 2>/dev/null | sed 's/claude-//g')"
    if r=$(running_ver) && [ "$r" != "$v" ]; then say "            the running service is still on $r; $v is used after a restart"; fi
    case "$m" in
    claude.ai) say "Login:      claude.ai" ;;
    "" | none) say "Login:      not logged in -> steward setup" ;;
    *) say "Login:      $m (Remote Control needs a claude.ai login) -> steward setup" ;;
    esac
  elif ! flock -n "$RUN_DIR/steward.download.lock" true 2>/dev/null; then
    say "Version:    Claude Code is downloading, please wait"
  else
    say "Version:    Claude Code not ready -> steward install"
    if [ -s "$INSTALL_LOG" ]; then
      say "  Last output of the installer:"
      strip_ansi <"$INSTALL_LOG" | grep -v '^[[:space:]]*$' | tail -n 3 | sed 's/^/    /'
    fi
  fi
  arr=$(sed -n 's/^mdState="\{0,1\}\([A-Z_]*\).*/\1/p' "$P/var/local/emhttp/var.ini" 2>/dev/null)
  say "Array:      ${arr:-unknown} (the service does not depend on the array)"
  say "Boot drive: $(df -h --output=avail "$P/boot" 2>/dev/null | tail -n1 | tr -d ' ') free"
  if ((PLUGIN)); then
    say "Install:    plugin $STEWARD_VERSION (managed on the Plugins page of the webGUI)"
  else
    say "Install:    script $STEWARD_VERSION"
    grep -qsF "$MARK" "$GO" || say "Boot hook:  the steward line is missing from $GO; it will not start automatically after a reboot -> steward install"
  fi
  url=$(cat "$LOG.1" "$LOG" 2>/dev/null | grep -a -o 'https://claude\.ai/code[^[:space:][:cntrl:]"]*' | tail -n1)
  [ -n "$url" ] && say "Last URL:   $url"
  ((smb)) && warn "The boot drive is currently shared over SMB and the login credentials are stored on it; disabling the share is recommended"
  say "Recent log:"
  tail -n 6 "$LOG" 2>/dev/null | strip_ansi | sed 's/^/  /'
  return 0
}

cmd_logs() {
  if [ "${1:-}" = "-f" ]; then tail -n 30 -f "$LOG"; else tail -n "${1:-60}" "$LOG" 2>/dev/null | strip_ansi; fi
}

do_uninstall() {
  rm -f "$ENABLED"
  stop_service 30
  rm -f "$CRON" "$BIN_DIR/claude" "$BIN_DIR/steward"
  rm -rf "$PLUGIN_DIR"
  [ -f "$GO" ] && sed -i "\|$MARK|d" "$GO"
  [ -f "$STOP" ] && sed -i "\|$MARK|d" "$STOP"
  [ -e "$P/usr/local/sbin/update_cron" ] && bash "$P/usr/local/sbin/update_cron" >/dev/null 2>&1
  say "Hooks removed and service stopped. The binary in RAM disappears after a reboot."
  say "Data (including login credentials) is still in $ROOT; delete it manually once you are sure you no longer need it."
}

cmd_uninstall() {
  ((PLUGIN)) && die "This was installed as a plugin: remove it on the Plugins page of the webGUI, or run plugin remove steward.plg"
  do_uninstall
}

# ───────────────────────── Plugin (steward.plg) entry points ─────────────────────────

webgui_up() { # Is emhttpd up yet. At boot the plugin loads before emhttpd, and nothing slow may be done in that phase
  [ -e "$P/var/local/emhttp/var.ini" ] || return 1
  [ -n "$P" ] || [ -n "$(pgrep --ns $$ -x emhttpd 2>/dev/null)" ]
}

cmd_plugin_install() { # The plugin manager runs this on every boot, and also when the plugin is installed or updated from the webGUI
  PLUGIN=1
  local booting=1 changed=0 problem
  webgui_up && booting=0
  if ((booting)); then
    # Failure must not be reported at boot: the plugin manager would move the .plg to plugins-error and never load it again on later boots
    if ! problem=$( (preflight) 2>&1); then
      log "Boot: preflight failed, not starting this time -- $problem"
      notify_user "Claude Code plugin did not start" "$problem"
      say "steward: $problem"
      return 0
    fi
  else
    preflight
  fi
  migrate_legacy
  mkdir -p "$CFG_DIR" "$WORKSPACE" "$CACHE" "$RUN_DIR" "${LOG%/*}"
  install_self && changed=1
  write_defaults
  drop_go_hook # If the script install was used before, go has a line; the plugin now loads it at boot instead
  stop_hook
  ensure_runtime
  if ((booting)); then
    # Restoring the binary takes seconds, and without a backup it has to be downloaded; push all of it to the background so boot is not held up
    (
      cd / || exit 1
      close_fds
      exec setsid bash "$SELF" boot </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1
    ) &
    say "steward $STEWARD_VERSION: loaded, service starting in the background"
    return 0
  fi
  say "steward $STEWARD_VERSION: plugin files in place"
  if ! need_binary; then
    warn "Plugin installed, but Claude Code has not downloaded successfully yet. Check the network, then press \"Start\" under Settings -> Steward, or run steward install in a terminal"
    return 0
  fi
  if sup_pid >/dev/null; then
    if ((changed)); then
      say "Script updated, restarting service to apply"
      if [ -n "${STEWARD_SVC:-}" ]; then
        # The plugin manager was started from a session inside the service (carrying the service tag): a direct restart would kill it too,
        # and its last step "copy the .plg to /boot/config/plugins" would never happen. Instead drop the tag, wait for it to finish, then restart
        (
          cd / || exit 1
          close_fds
          exec env -u "$TAG" setsid bash -c 'sleep 10; exec bash "$0" restart' "$SELF" </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1
        ) &
        say "(Triggered from a session inside the service: restarting in the background in 10 seconds; Remote Control will drop briefly)"
      else
        cmd_restart
      fi
    else
      say "Service running, no restart needed"
    fi
  elif [ -f "$ENABLED" ]; then
    cmd_start
  elif [ "$(auth_method)" = "claude.ai" ]; then
    say "Next step: press \"Start\" under Settings -> Steward. If the first-time Remote Control setup has not been done yet, press \"Sign in & first-time setup\" first"
  else
    say "Next step: press \"Sign in & first-time setup\" under Settings -> Steward, or run steward setup in a terminal"
  fi
  boot_shared_over_smb && warn "The boot drive is currently shared over SMB, and the login credentials are stored on it. On the Main page, open the boot device and set Export under SMB Security Settings to No"
  return 0
}

cmd_plugin_remove() { do_uninstall; }

# Backend of the settings page (Settings -> Steward). Actions triggered by buttons always run in the background and the page polls for progress every second,
# so no web request ever waits until it times out, and progress is visible even for things that take minutes, like a download.

job_busy() { [ -e "$JOBLOCK" ] && ! flock -n "$JOBLOCK" true 2>/dev/null; }

cmd_gui() {
  local what=${1:-} arg=${2:-} sock p i
  case "$what" in
  status | poll) # The first lines are state for the page to parse, followed by the job log; status also appends the full status
    if job_busy; then say "busy 1"; else say "busy 0"; fi
    if sup_pid >/dev/null; then say "service 1"; else say "service 0"; fi
    if [ -f "$ENABLED" ]; then say "enabled 1"; else say "enabled 0"; fi
    say "--- job"
    if [ -s "$JOBLOG" ]; then
      strip_ansi <"$JOBLOG"
      [ -z "$(tail -c1 "$JOBLOG")" ] || say ""
    fi
    if [ "$what" = status ]; then
      say "--- status"
      cmd_status 2>&1
    fi
    ;;
  run)
    case "$arg" in start | stop | restart) ;; *) die "Unsupported action: $arg" ;; esac
    mkdir -p "$RUN_DIR"
    exec 7>"$JOBLOCK"
    if ! flock -n 7; then
      say "busy"
      return 0
    fi
    : >"$JOBLOG"
    ( # The lock (fd 7) is handed to the background job and released only when it exits
      cd / || exit 1
      close_fds 7
      exec setsid bash "$SELF" _gui job "$arg" </dev/null >>"$JOBLOG" 2>&1
    ) &
    exec 7>&-
    say "started"
    ;;
  job)
    case "$arg" in
    start) cmd_start ;;
    stop) cmd_stop ;;
    restart) cmd_restart ;;
    esac
    ;;
  terminal) # "Sign in & first-time setup": open a web terminal that only runs setup, at the URL /logterminal/steward/
    sock="$P/var/tmp/steward.sock"
    command -v ttyd-exec >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "This machine has no ttyd-exec; run steward setup in a terminal instead"
    for p in $(pgrep --ns $$ -x ttyd 2>/dev/null); do # If the window from last time is still open, end it first and wait until it is really gone before opening a new one
      [ -r "/proc/$p/cmdline" ] || continue
      tr '\0' '\n' <"/proc/$p/cmdline" 2>/dev/null | grep -qxF -- "$sock" || continue
      kill "$p" 2>/dev/null
      for ((i = 0; i < 20; i++)); do
        [ -d "/proc/$p" ] || break
        sleep 0.1
      done
    done
    mkdir -p "${sock%/*}"
    rm -f "$sock"
    ttyd-exec -om1 -i "$sock" bash "$SELF" _gui setup
    for ((i = 0; i < 30; i++)); do
      [ -S "$sock" ] && break
      sleep 0.1
    done
    [ -S "$sock" ] || die "The terminal did not start; run steward setup in the Unraid terminal instead"
    say "ok"
    ;;
  setup) # What runs inside that terminal window
    HOLD_WINDOW=1
    cmd_setup
    ;;
  *) return 2 ;;
  esac
}

# ───────────────────────── Optional skills ─────────────────────────
# Guides for Claude (array, docs, tools, health checks) that a user can opt into. A fresh install ships none of them:
# the plugin carries the files under $PLUGIN_DIR/skills, and "steward skills install" copies one into the config dir,
# where Claude Code picks it up at the next session. Script installs have no plugin dir and download from the repository.
SKILLS_SRC="$PLUGIN_DIR/skills"
SKILLS_DST="$CFG_DIR/skills"
SKILLS_RAW="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/hom-wang/steward/main/skills"
SKILL_NAMES="unraid unraid-docs unraid-tools unraid-health" # must match the skills/ directory in the repository (tests check)

skill_source() { # $1=name $2=download timeout in seconds. Prints the path of a SKILL.md to install from: the shipped copy, else a downloaded temp file
  if [ -f "$SKILLS_SRC/$1/SKILL.md" ]; then
    printf '%s\n' "$SKILLS_SRC/$1/SKILL.md"
    return 0
  fi
  local t
  t=$(mktemp "/tmp/.steward-install.$$.skill.XXXXXX") || return 1 # the cleanup trap removes /tmp/.steward-install.$$.* on any exit
  # Accept the download only if it looks like one of our skills: a captive portal or proxy page also answers 200
  if curl -fsSL --max-time "${2:-30}" "$SKILLS_RAW/$1/SKILL.md" -o "$t" 2>/dev/null && [ "$(head -n1 "$t")" = "---" ] &&
    grep -qx "name: $1" "$t" && [ -n "$(skill_meta "$t" skill-version)" ]; then
    printf '%s\n' "$t"
  else
    rm -f "$t"
    return 1
  fi
}
skill_meta() { # $1=SKILL.md $2=front-matter key (skill-version, unraid-verified, unraid-applies)
  sed -n '2,/^---$/p' "$1" | sed -n "s/^[[:space:]]*$2:[[:space:]]*\"\{0,1\}\([^\"]*\)\"\{0,1\}[[:space:]]*$/\1/p" | head -n1
}
skill_known() { # $1=name. Only names from the fixed list, so nothing outside $SKILLS_DST/<name> can ever be touched
  case "$1" in '' | *[!a-z0-9-]*) return 1 ;; esac
  case " $SKILL_NAMES " in *" $1 "*) return 0 ;; esac
  return 1
}
skill_tmp_done() { case "$1" in /tmp/.steward-install.*) rm -f "$1" ;; esac; }

cmd_skills() {
  local sub=${1:-list} name f dst state
  [ $# -gt 0 ] && shift
  case "$sub" in
  list)
    say "Optional skills. Install: steward skills install <name>   Remove: steward skills remove <name>"
    say "Installed skills live in $SKILLS_DST and are read by Claude Code at the start of each session."
    [ -d "$SKILLS_SRC" ] || say "(no shipped copies on this install; versions below come from a quick download and may be missing offline)"
    for name in $SKILL_NAMES; do
      state="not installed"
      if [ -f "$SKILLS_DST/$name/SKILL.md" ]; then state="installed $(skill_meta "$SKILLS_DST/$name/SKILL.md" skill-version)"; fi
      if f=$(skill_source "$name" 8); then
        if [ -f "$SKILLS_DST/$name/SKILL.md" ] && ! cmp -s "$f" "$SKILLS_DST/$name/SKILL.md"; then
          if [ "$(skill_meta "$f" skill-version)" = "$(skill_meta "$SKILLS_DST/$name/SKILL.md" skill-version)" ]; then
            state="$state, differs from the shipped copy (edited locally?)"
          else
            state="$state, update available $(skill_meta "$f" skill-version)"
          fi
        fi
        printf '  %-14s %-12s verified %-7s applies %-10s %s\n' "$name" "$(skill_meta "$f" skill-version)" "$(skill_meta "$f" unraid-verified)" "$(skill_meta "$f" unraid-applies)" "$state"
        skill_tmp_done "$f"
      else
        printf '  %-14s %-12s %-35s %s\n' "$name" "?" "(not available offline)" "$state"
      fi
    done
    ;;
  install)
    [ $# -ge 1 ] || die "usage: steward skills install <name> [<name> ...]   (names: $SKILL_NAMES)"
    for name in "$@"; do
      skill_known "$name" || die "unknown skill: $name (steward skills list)"
      f=$(skill_source "$name" 30) || die "cannot get $name: not shipped with this install and the download failed"
      dst=$SKILLS_DST/$name
      mkdir -p "$dst" || die "cannot create $dst"
      if [ -f "$dst/SKILL.md" ] && ! cmp -s "$f" "$dst/SKILL.md"; then
        cp -p "$dst/SKILL.md" "$dst/SKILL.md.before-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)" || die "cannot back up the existing $dst/SKILL.md"
        say "$name: existing copy differs, kept as SKILL.md.before-<timestamp>"
      fi
      cp "$f" "$dst/SKILL.md" && chmod 644 "$dst/SKILL.md" || die "cannot write $dst/SKILL.md"
      say "$name $(skill_meta "$dst/SKILL.md" skill-version) installed (verified on Unraid $(skill_meta "$dst/SKILL.md" unraid-verified)); new sessions will see it"
      skill_tmp_done "$f"
    done
    ;;
  remove)
    [ $# -ge 1 ] || die "usage: steward skills remove <name> [<name> ...]"
    for name in "$@"; do
      skill_known "$name" || die "unknown skill: $name"
      if [ -d "$SKILLS_DST/$name" ]; then
        rm -rf "${SKILLS_DST:?}/$name" && say "$name removed (including its SKILL.md.before-* backups)"
      else
        say "$name is not installed"
      fi
    done
    rmdir "$SKILLS_DST" 2>/dev/null || true # back to the clean state when nothing is left
    ;;
  *) die "usage: steward skills [list | install <name> ... | remove <name> ...]" ;;
  esac
}

usage() {
  cat <<'EOF'
steward <command>
  install      First install or repair: create directories and hooks, prepare Claude Code (not needed manually for plugin installs)
  setup        Log in to claude.ai and complete the first-time Remote Control confirmation (needs an interactive terminal)
  start        Start the resident service and enable start at boot
  stop         Stop the service and disable start at boot
  restart      Restart the service (use after editing steward.conf)
  status       Service, login, version, last connection URL
  logs [-f|N]  Show the log
  update       Update Claude Code now (normally updated automatically daily after 08:00 when no session is in use)
  uninstall    Remove hooks and stop the service, keeping the data (for plugin installs, remove it on the Plugins page)
  skills       Optional guides for Claude: list, install <name>, remove <name>. Nothing is installed by default
EOF
}

cmd=${1:-help}
[ $# -gt 0 ] && shift

# These commands end every process under the service. If Claude inside the service called them itself, run them outside the service instead, so it does not kill itself halfway through
if [ -n "${STEWARD_SVC:-}" ]; then
  case "$cmd" in
  start | stop | restart | update | uninstall | watchdog) # watchdog may trigger the restart in auto_update
    log "$cmd requested from inside the service, handing it to the background"
    (
      cd / || exit 1
      close_fds
      exec env -u "$TAG" setsid bash "$SELF" "$cmd" "$@" </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1
    ) &
    say "$cmd handed to the background. If the service restarts because of it, the Remote Control connection will drop briefly."
    exit 0
    ;;
  esac
fi

case "$cmd" in
install) cmd_install ;;
setup) cmd_setup ;;
start) cmd_start ;;
stop) cmd_stop ;;
restart) cmd_restart ;;
status) cmd_status ;;
logs) cmd_logs "$@" ;;
update) cmd_update ;;
uninstall) cmd_uninstall ;;
boot) cmd_boot ;;
shutdown) cmd_shutdown ;;
watchdog) cmd_watchdog ;;
_supervise) supervise ;;
_event) on_array_stop ;;
_plugin_install) cmd_plugin_install ;;
_plugin_remove) cmd_plugin_remove ;;
_gui) cmd_gui "$@" ;;
skills) cmd_skills "$@" ;;
*) usage ;;
esac
]]></INLINE>
</FILE>

<!-- 6. Optional skills: guides for Claude. Not installed by default; "steward skills install <name>" copies one into /boot/steward/home/skills -->
<FILE Name="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/steward/skills/unraid-docs/SKILL.md">
<INLINE><![CDATA[
---
name: unraid-docs
description: How to find correct, current information about Unraid for the exact version installed on this server, and how to tell outdated or wrong advice from the real thing. Use before acting on any claim about how Unraid works that you have not verified on this machine, when a guide or skill was written for a different Unraid version, when the owner asks "how do I" about a feature, and after Unraid itself has been updated.
metadata:
  skill-version: "2026.10.23"
  unraid-verified: "7.3.3"
  unraid-applies: "7.x"
---

# Finding the right Unraid information

Unraid changes between releases: paths move, GUI pages are renamed, features appear (ZFS, Internal Boot, the API) and old advice keeps circulating on forums and blogs. The failure mode to avoid is applying a procedure written for one version to a server running another. This skill is about establishing what is true for *this* server.

## 1. Always start from the installed version

```
cat /etc/unraid-version
findmnt -no FSTYPE /boot     # vfat = USB boot stick, zfs = Internal Boot
grep -E '^(mdState|fsState)=' /var/local/emhttp/var.ini
```

Write the version down in your working notes and compare it with whatever you read next. A guide that names a different major or minor version (6.12 versus 7.3) is a hypothesis until confirmed. A release candidate (`-rc`) may differ from the stable notes.

Every Steward skill carries `unraid-verified` (the version it was checked on) and `unraid-applies` (the range it is believed to hold for) in its front matter. If the installed version is outside that range, treat the skill's version-specific claims as unverified and confirm them here before acting.

## 2. Sources, in order of trust

**1. The machine itself.** For "what does this version actually do", nothing beats reading the code that runs it:

- `/usr/local/emhttp/languages/en_US/helptext.txt`: the help text shown by the "?" buttons in the webGUI, about 2,800 lines, organised as `:<page>_help:` ... `:end` blocks. Search it first for any setting: `grep -n -i -A 12 'mover' /usr/local/emhttp/languages/en_US/helptext.txt`.
- `/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/dynamix/*.page`: the webGUI pages themselves. The file name matches the page title (`ArrayOperation.page`, `BootParameters.page`, `ShareEdit.page`). They show which settings exist, their allowed values and which script a button runs.
- `/usr/local/emhttp/webGui/scripts/` (`webGui` is a symlink to `plugins/dynamix`, so this is the same directory as `plugins/dynamix/scripts/`): the scripts behind the GUI (`notify`, `diagnostics`, `manage_boot_params.sh`, `parity_control`, `flash_backup`). Unraid's own command-line tools are in `/usr/local/sbin/` (`update_cron`, `mover`, `newperms`, `sdspin`, `plugin`). Read a script before relying on a forum description of it.
- `/etc/rc.d/rc.*`: service start scripts and the boot sequence (`rc.S`, `rc.local`, `rc.6`). They are the authority on what is read from `/boot/config` and when.
- `/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/*/README.md` and each plugin's `.plg` in `/boot/config/plugins/`: what installed plugins do and which version they are.

Use these to confirm a path or command exists before running it: `ls`, `grep -n`, `bash -n`. This is cheap and removes most version mistakes.

**2. Official documentation: https://docs.unraid.net/** The content is organised in these sections (directory names in the docs repository; the section index URLs themselves may return 404, individual pages under them work):

- `unraid-os/getting-started/`, `unraid-os/using-unraid-to/` (shares, Docker, VMs, Community Applications), `unraid-os/system-administration/` (security, networking, backups, the boot device), `unraid-os/troubleshooting/`, `unraid-os/updating-unraid/`, `unraid-os/advanced-configurations/`.
- `unraid-os/release-notes/<version>/`, one page per release, for example `https://docs.unraid.net/unraid-os/release-notes/7.3.3/`. This is where behaviour changes are announced; read the notes for every release between the version a guide was written for and the installed one. The repository may already hold notes for releases newer than the installed one: check the version in the page title.
- `https://docs.unraid.net/API/` (and `API/cli/`): the GraphQL API and the `unraid-api` CLI (Unraid 7.2 and later).
- `https://docs.unraid.net/connect/`: Connect, remote access and automated Flash Backup.

The docs are built from the public repository https://github.com/unraid/docs (branch `main`, pages under `docs/unraid-os/...` as Markdown). When a page is long, the site is slow, or a section index 404s, fetch the Markdown source instead: list a directory with `https://api.github.com/repos/unraid/docs/contents/docs/unraid-os/<section>` to get exact file names, then read the raw file.

**3. Unraid's own code on GitHub.** https://github.com/unraid/webgui is the webGUI (the same files as in `/usr/local/emhttp`, with history, so you can see when something changed and in which release). https://github.com/unraid/api is the API and Connect plugin. Use these to answer "since which version" questions.

**4. The forums: https://forums.unraid.net/** Release announcements and the pinned posts in Announcements are authoritative for their date. Plugin support threads are the right place for plugin behaviour. Everything else is user posts: check the post date and the version the poster mentions, and expect answers from the 6.x era to be partly wrong on 7.x (ZFS, pools in the array, Internal Boot, the API, the new Community Apps submission flow all arrived later).

**5. Blogs, videos, Reddit, AI summaries.** Lowest trust. Use them to discover that a feature exists, then confirm it in sources 1 and 2.

## 3. How to fetch

- Official docs pages and GitHub raw files: fetch them directly and read the text. Prefer the release-notes page for the installed version over a general guide.
- Searching: restrict to `docs.unraid.net` or `forums.unraid.net` when possible, and include the version number in the query ("7.3 mover settings").
- Forum pages are large; fetch the specific topic URL rather than a search page, and look for the first post and the newest posts.
- Do not download and run anything from these sources. Read, then apply by hand with the usual backups and consent rules.

## 4. Before acting on what you found

1. Does the procedure name files, pages or commands? Confirm each exists on this server (source 1).
2. Does it touch `/boot/config`, the array, pools, boot parameters or disk assignments? Apply the consent and backup rules from the `unraid` guide regardless of what the source says.
3. Does it describe a USB-boot system while this one is Internal Boot, or the reverse? Boot-device procedures differ (FAT versus ZFS, syslinux versus GRUB); do not mix them.
4. Was it written for a different major.minor? Read the release notes in between and look for the feature's name.
5. Can you verify the outcome with a read-only check afterwards? Decide what that check is before you start.

## 5. Record what you verified

When you confirm something version-specific, note it where the next session will find it: the installed version, the date, the source, and what was confirmed or contradicted. Phrase it so a later reader on a newer version knows it needs re-checking, for example "verified on 7.3.3 (2026-10-23): udev rules in /boot/config/udev are copied at boot by rc.S.cont". If a Steward skill turned out to be wrong for this version, say so to the owner and suggest updating the skill rather than working around it silently.
]]></INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Name="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/steward/skills/unraid-health/SKILL.md">
<INLINE><![CDATA[
---
name: unraid-health
description: Run a read-only health check of an Unraid server (array, parity, disks and SMART, pools, boot device, memory, log partition, Docker, recent errors) and report it as a short table. Use when asked how the server is doing, before risky maintenance, or when something feels slow or wrong.
metadata:
  skill-version: "2026.10.23"
  unraid-verified: "7.3.3"
  unraid-applies: "7.x"
---

# Unraid health check

Everything here is read-only. None of these commands change state, spin up sleeping disks, or need consent. Run them all, then report in the format at the end.

**Version check first:** `cat /etc/unraid-version`. The field names in `var.ini` and `disks.ini` below were verified on 7.3.3. On another major version, confirm a field exists (`grep -c '^mdState=' /var/local/emhttp/var.ini`) before reading it, and consult the `unraid-docs` skill if it does not.

## 1. Array and parity

```
grep -E '^(mdState|fsState|mdNumDisks|mdNumInvalid|mdNumMissing|mdNumDisabled|mdResync|mdResyncAction|mdResyncPos|mdResyncSize|mdResyncDt|mdResyncDb|sbSynced|sbSyncErrs|sbSyncExit|shareMoverActive)=' /var/local/emhttp/var.ini
```

- Healthy: `mdState=STARTED`, `fsState=Started`, and no degraded slot in section 2. Do not judge degradation from `mdNumInvalid`/`mdNumDisabled`/`mdNumMissing`: an empty parity slot raises those counters on a healthy array. `/proc/mdstat` holds the same `key=value` data if `var.ini` is stale.
- Parity check or rebuild in progress: `mdResync` greater than 0. Progress is `mdResyncPos / mdResyncSize`; `mdResyncAction` tells check versus rebuild, and `P` or `Q` means which parity.
- Last parity check: `sbSynced` is its start and `sbSynced2` its end, both Unix timestamps (`date -d @<value>`); `sbSyncErrs` is the error count, `sbSyncExit` the exit code (0 means it finished). A `sbSyncErrs` above 0 is worth flagging even if everything else looks fine.
- `fsNumUnmountable` above 0: a disk or pool did not mount; section 2 shows which.
- `shareMoverActive=yes`: the mover is running, which explains disk activity and slow shares.

## 2. Disks

```
awk -F= '/^\[/{d=$0} /^(device|status|color|rotational|temp|numErrors|fsStatus|fsFree|fsSize|spundown|warning|critical)=/{print d, $0}' /var/local/emhttp/disks.ini
```

- Judge each slot the way the webGUI does, from `status` and `color` together. `status=DISK_OK` with `color=green-on` is good. A `status` containing `_MISSING`, `DISK_DSBL` or `DISK_INVALID`, or a `color` starting with `red-` or `yellow-`, is a missing, disabled or emulated disk: the array is degraded, say so first. `status=DISK_NP_DSBL` with `color=grey-off` and an empty `device` is simply an unused slot (typically a second parity slot) and is not a problem.
- `numErrors` above 0: read errors seen by the md driver since boot. Check syslog for `md: disk<N> read error` and look at SMART.
- `temp` is Celsius; `*` means spun down. Thresholds are in `[display]` of `/boot/config/plugins/dynamix/dynamix.cfg`: `hot`/`max` for spinning disks (defaults 45/55) and `hotssd`/`maxssd` for SSD and NVMe (defaults 60/70); pick the pair from `rotational=` in `disks.ini`. A non-empty `warning`/`critical` on a disk overrides the global values for that disk. Read the thresholds before calling any temperature high.
- `fsStatus=Mounted` for every data disk and pool when the array is started. `Unmountable` is a problem, usually a filesystem that needs a check from the webGUI (Main -> disk -> Check Filesystem), never from the shell.
- Free space: compute `fsFree / fsSize` per disk. Flag anything under 5 percent; the share `floor` setting can make writes fail before 0.

**SMART, without waking sleeping disks.** `emhttpd` already keeps a recent SMART report per slot in `/var/local/emhttp/smart/<slot>`; read those first:

```
for f in /var/local/emhttp/smart/*; do echo "== $f ($(stat -c %y "$f" | cut -c1-16))"; command grep -E 'overall-health|SMART Health|Reallocated_Sector|Reported_Uncorrect|Current_Pending|Offline_Uncorrectable|UDMA_CRC|Percentage Used|Media and Data|Temperature' "$f"; done
```

Only for a device with no cached report, query it directly with `smartctl -n standby -H -A /dev/sdX`: `-n standby` prints a short notice and skips a disk that is spun down; report it as "not checked (spun down)" rather than waking it. `sdspin sdX status` (exit code 2 = spun down) or `hdparm -C /dev/sdX` shows the power state without touching the platters. The attributes to watch: 5 Reallocated, 187 Reported Uncorrectable, 197 Current Pending, 198 Offline Uncorrectable (any increase is bad), 199 UDMA CRC (cabling), and for NVMe "Percentage Used" and "Media and Data Integrity Errors".

## 3. Pools

```
zpool status -x 2>/dev/null; zpool list -o name,size,alloc,free,cap,health 2>/dev/null
btrfs filesystem show 2>/dev/null
for p in /mnt/*/; do case "$p" in /mnt/user/|/mnt/user0/|/mnt/disk*/|/mnt/addons/|/mnt/remotes/|/mnt/rootshare/) ;; *) btrfs device stats "$p" 2>/dev/null | grep -v ' 0$';; esac; done
```

- ZFS: `all pools are healthy` is the answer you want. Anything else, show `zpool status` for that pool and say whether a scrub is running or a device is faulted.
- btrfs: `btrfs device stats` lines that are not zero are accumulated errors; corruption and generation errors matter most.
- Capacity: a pool above 90 percent slows down and can stall Docker and VMs if appdata or the Docker image lives there.

## 4. Boot device, memory, log partition

```
df -h /boot /var/log /
free -m
uptime
ls -laS /var/log | head -n 5
```

- `/var/log` is a small RAM filesystem. Above 80 percent, find what is filling it (the `ls -laS` line) before it hits 100 and services start failing silently.
- `/` (RAM root) filling up usually means something is writing to a path that is not under `/mnt` or `/boot`, often a container with a bad volume mapping or downloads going to `/tmp`.
- Memory: Unraid uses free RAM as page cache, so low "free" is normal; look at `available` and at swap (there usually is none). A ZFS ARC can hold a large share; `awk '/^(size|c_max) /{printf "%s %.1f GiB\n", $1, $3/1073741824}' /proc/spl/kstat/zfs/arcstats` shows its current size and cap (there is no `arc_summary` on Unraid).
- Load average far above the core count with no parity check running points at a stuck process on `/mnt`; `ps -eo pid,stat,wchan:20,cmd | grep ' D '` lists processes in uninterruptible wait.

## 5. Docker and VMs

```
docker ps -a --format 'table {{.Names}}\t{{.Status}}' 2>/dev/null
docker stats --no-stream --format 'table {{.Name}}\t{{.CPUPerc}}\t{{.MemUsage}}' 2>/dev/null
df -h /var/lib/docker 2>/dev/null
docker system df 2>/dev/null
virsh list --all 2>/dev/null
```

- Containers that show `Restarting`, `Exited (non-zero)` or `Created` (never started, or failed to start) are the ones to look at: `docker logs --tail 50 <name>`.
- `df -h /var/lib/docker` is the fill level of the Docker image or directory (its size is set in Settings -> Docker). Above 80 percent, say so; the fix is the owner's (bigger image, or a container writing inside itself). `docker system df` only tells what is reclaimable.

## 6. Recent errors in the log

```
grep -iE 'error|fail|warn|call trace|oom|read error|segfault' /var/log/syslog | grep -viE 'steward|audit|ntpd' | tail -n 40
```

Group what you find by subsystem (md, filesystem, network, docker, kernel). Do not paste raw log walls; quote the two or three lines that explain a problem and say when they happened.

## 7. Report format

Lead with the verdict in one line: "All healthy", or "Attention: <the one or two things that matter>". Then a table:

| Area | State | Note |
|---|---|---|
| Array | STARTED, 0 invalid/missing | last parity check <date>, 0 errors |
| Disks | all DISK_OK, max 38 C | disk3 at 92 percent full |
| SMART | no reallocated/pending sectors | disk5 spun down, not checked |
| Pools | cache: healthy, 61 percent | |
| Boot / logs | /boot 28G free, /var/log 12 percent | |
| Memory / load | 9.1G available, load 0.8 | |
| Docker / VMs | 14 running, 1 exited | nginx exited (1) 2 h ago |
| Syslog | 3 warnings | all from ntpd, harmless |

Finish with the actions you recommend, each marked as "I can do this now", "needs your OK", or "webGUI only". Do not start any of them inside the health check.
]]></INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Name="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/steward/skills/unraid-tools/SKILL.md">
<INLINE><![CDATA[
---
name: unraid-tools
description: Which command-line tools exist on a stock Unraid server and which do not, with full paths for the Unraid-specific ones, so you can pick a working tool instead of probing for it. Use when deciding how to parse, fetch, inspect or script something, and before assuming a tool such as python, nc or tmux is present.
metadata:
  skill-version: "2026.10.23"
  unraid-verified: "7.3.3"
  unraid-applies: "7.x"
---

# Tools available on Unraid

Unraid is a Slackware-based appliance. The installed tool set is fixed by the release, lives in RAM, and cannot be extended persistently with a package manager. What is listed as present below was checked with `command -v` on Unraid 7.3.3. On another version, a quick `command -v <tool>` settles it; the installed package list is `ls /var/log/packages`.

## Present and safe to rely on

**Shell and text.** `bash` 5.3 (`/bin/sh` is bash), GNU coreutils, `gawk` (as `awk`), GNU `sed`, GNU `grep` 3.12 (with `-P`) and `pcregrep`, `find`, `xargs`, `sort`, `diff`, `patch`, `less`, `nano`, `vi` (which is `elvis`, not vim), `file`, `fromdos`/`todos` (no `dos2unix`), `base64`, `md5sum`/`sha256sum`, `openssl`, `timeout`, `flock`, `setsid`, `nohup`, `watch`, `logger`.

**About `grep` inside Claude Code.** In Claude Code's Bash tool, plain `grep` is a shell function that runs a bundled `ugrep`, not GNU grep. It rejects moderately complex patterns ("exceeds complexity limits"), skips binary files, and honours ignore files. For `-P`, long alternations, binary files (`-a`) or anything that behaves oddly, call GNU grep explicitly: `command grep ...` or `/bin/grep ...`.

**Data formats.** `jq` 1.8 for JSON, `xmllint` for XML (validate with `xmllint --noout`, query with `--xpath`), `php` 8.4 CLI (the webGUI language, handy for INI parsing: `php -r '$v = parse_ini_file("/var/local/emhttp/var.ini"); echo $v["mdState"], "\n";'`; never dump `var.ini` whole, it contains `csrf_token` and registration GUIDs), `perl` 5. `node` 22 and `npm` are present because the built-in Unraid API ships them (they arrive with the `dynamix.unraid.net` package, not as a Slackware package); fine for one-off scripts, but not something to build on for older versions.

**Archives.** `tar`, `gzip`, `bzip2`, `xz`, `zstd`, `lz4`, `zip`/`unzip`, `cpio`. No `7z`, no `pigz`.

**Network.** `curl` 8 (with HTTPS), `wget`, `ssh`/`scp`/`rsync`, `ip`, `ss`, `netstat`, `ping`, `traceroute`, `dig`/`nslookup`/`host`, `ethtool`, `arp`, `iptables`, `wg` (WireGuard), `avahi-browse`, `smbclient`, `showmount`. Nothing for packet capture or port scanning.

**Disks and filesystems.** `lsblk`, `blkid`, `smartctl` 7.5 (`-n standby` to avoid waking disks), `hdparm`, `sdparm`, `nvme`, `zpool`/`zfs` 2.4, `btrfs` 7, `xfs_info`, `xfs_repair`, `cryptsetup`, `lsof`, `fuser`, `df`, `du`. The destructive ones (`mkfs.*`, `wipefs`, `sgdisk`, `fdisk`, `parted`, `xfs_repair` without `-n`) also exist; they are never to be used by you, and the shipped bash-guard hook blocks the worst of them.

**System.** `htop`, `top`, `iotop`, `iostat`, `vmstat`, `free`, `uptime`, `sensors`, `lscpu`, `lspci`, `lsusb`, `dmidecode`, `mcelog`, `at`, `crontab` (but see cron below).

**Containers and VMs.** `docker` 29 (no `docker compose` plugin by default), `containerd`, `runc`, `virsh`, `qemu-img`.

**Version control.** `git` 2.56. No `gh`.

## Unraid-specific commands

On `PATH` (`/usr/local/sbin`, `/usr/local/bin`):

| Command | What it is | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `mdcmd` | array driver control | `mdcmd status` is read-only; anything else changes array state and needs consent. Prefer reading `/proc/mdstat` or `var.ini`. |
| `emcmd` | send a command to `emhttpd` the way the GUI does | Same caution as `mdcmd`. |
| `plugin` | plugin manager | `plugin install <file or URL>`, `plugin check <name>.plg`, `plugin remove <name>.plg`, `plugin update <name>.plg`. Installing or removing plugins needs consent. |
| `unraid-api` | the GraphQL API CLI (7.2 and later) | `unraid-api --help`; API keys via `unraid-api apikey`. |
| `diagnostics` | builds the diagnostics zip into `/boot/logs/` | Read-only; takes a minute. |
| `mover` | the mover | `mover status` is safe; `mover start` moves data and should be the owner's call. |
| `update_cron` | rebuilds the single crontab `/etc/cron.d/root` from `/boot/config/plugins/dynamix/*.cron` and the `.cron` files of installed plugins | Run after adding a `.cron` file. Anything else in `/etc/cron.d` is discarded. |
| `newperms` | the "New Permissions" tool | Recursive chown/chmod; owner only. |
| `sdspin` | Unraid's spin state helper: `sdspin sdX status` (exit 2 = spun down, ATA only), `sdspin sdX up|down` | Status is safe; up/down changes state. |
| `powerdown` | deprecated wrapper around `/sbin/init 0` (`-r` reboots) | All shutdown commands end in `/etc/rc.d/rc.6`, which stops the array and runs `/boot/config/stop`. Consent required; prefer the webGUI. |

Not on `PATH`, call by full path (`webGui` is a symlink to `plugins/dynamix`, so both spellings reach the same scripts):

| Path | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `/usr/local/emhttp/webGui/scripts/notify` | create an Unraid notification: `notify -e "Steward" -s "<subject>" -d "<text>" -i normal` |
| `/usr/local/emhttp/webGui/scripts/parity_control` | pause/resume a running parity check (`pause`, `resume`); consent required |
| `/usr/local/emhttp/webGui/scripts/flash_backup` | the Main -> Flash "Flash backup" button: zips the whole boot device, by default into `/` (RAM); owner's tool, the zip contains secrets |
| `/usr/local/emhttp/webGui/scripts/reload_services` | internal helper: runs service `update` steps only when the webGUI queued a job with `at`; a no-op otherwise, so not a way to apply hand edits |
| `/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/dynamix/scripts/manage_boot_params.sh` | boot parameters (Internal Boot and USB); consent required, never edit grub.cfg by hand |

To apply a hand edit: `/etc/rc.d/rc.samba reload` after `smb-extra.conf`, `/etc/rc.d/rc.nfsd reload` for NFS exports. Share settings (`shares/*.cfg`) have no command-line reload; they are webGUI only.

The rest of `/usr/local/emhttp/webGui/scripts/` are the scripts behind GUI buttons. Read them before using them; most assume the GUI's context.

## Absent (do not try)

`python`/`python3`, `ruby`, `lua`, `bc`, `yq`, `xmlstarlet`, `dos2unix`, `vim` (use `nano` or `elvis`), `tmux`/`screen`, `nc`/`netcat`, `nmap`, `tcpdump`, `mtr`, `nft`, `strace`, `gdb`, `shellcheck`, `gh`, `ncdu`, `docker-compose`, `virt-install`, `sshpass`, `strings`.

Needs that would normally call for one of these:

- Arithmetic: bash `$(( ))`, `awk`, or `jq -n`.
- YAML: there is no parser; treat it as text, or use `node` with care.
- Text extraction from binaries: `grep -a` or `od`.
- Terminal multiplexing or detaching a long job: `setsid nohup <cmd> >log 2>&1 &`, or `at`.
- Port checks: `curl -sS --connect-timeout 3 host:port`, `ss -ltnp`, or `bash -c '</dev/tcp/host/port'`.
- Downloading and running tools: a static binary dropped under `/usr/local/bin` works until reboot. Persisting it means a `go` entry or a plugin, which is the owner's decision. Community plugins (NerdTools, un-get) add packages the same way.

## Things that behave differently from a desktop Linux

- `cron`: dcron. `update_cron` writes one crontab, `/etc/cron.d/root`, from the `.cron` files under `/boot/config/plugins/`; other files in `/etc/cron.d` and `crontab -e` edits are lost on the next run or reboot.
- `sudo` exists but you are already root; there are no other interactive users by default.
- No `systemctl`. Services are `/etc/rc.d/rc.<name> start|stop|restart|status`.
- `/tmp`, `/var/log`, `/root`, `/usr/local/bin` are RAM. `/boot` and `/mnt` are the only places that persist.
- Locales: `C`, `POSIX` and `en_US.UTF-8` only. `C.UTF-8` does not exist, and a shell that exports it makes `perl` and `virsh` print locale warnings. Use `LANG=en_US.UTF-8` for UTF-8 work.
- `/proc/mdstat` is Unraid's `key=value` array status, not the standard Linux md format.
- Docker's data root is a loopback image or directory on a pool (see `/boot/config/docker.cfg`), mounted at `/var/lib/docker`; `df -h /var/lib/docker` shows how full it is.
]]></INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Name="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/steward/skills/unraid/SKILL.md">
<INLINE><![CDATA[
---
name: unraid
description: Operating guide for working as a root admin assistant on an Unraid server. Use whenever a task touches the array, pools, shares, Docker, VMs, /boot/config, boot parameters, notifications, logs or the Unraid API, before deciding how to do it or whether to ask first.
metadata:
  skill-version: "2026.10.23"
  unraid-verified: "7.3.3"
  unraid-applies: "7.2 - 7.3"
---

# Unraid operating guide

You are running directly on the host as root. There is no container around you. Everything here is about doing the job without breaking the array, losing data, or leaving the server in a state the owner cannot recover from remotely.

## 0. Check the version first

This guide was verified on Unraid 7.3.3 and is written for 7.2 through 7.3. Before relying on it:

```
cat /etc/unraid-version
```

If the installed version is outside that range, or newer than the `unraid-verified` value above, treat every path, file name and GUI location here as a hypothesis: confirm it on the machine or in the release notes with the `unraid-docs` skill before acting. Things known to differ in older releases: no Unraid API before 7.2, no `go.safemode` or `/boot/config/udev` handling before 7.2, no Internal Boot on 6.x.

## 1. How the system is put together

**Root filesystem lives in RAM.** Unraid boots from the boot device, unpacks the OS into RAM, and runs from there. Anything you change outside `/boot` and `/mnt` is gone at the next reboot: packages, files in `/etc`, `/usr`, `/root`, cron entries, users. Persistent configuration has two homes:

- `/boot/config/`: Unraid's own configuration. Read at boot, partly rewritten by the webGUI.
- `/boot/config/go`: a shell script run late in boot. The traditional place to re-apply customisations. There is also `go.safemode` (run instead of `go` in safe mode) and `stop` (run at shutdown).

**The boot device.** Either a USB stick (FAT, `/boot` mounted with limited permissions) or, on newer systems, Internal Boot (a ZFS pool mounted at `/boot`). Check with `stat -f -c %T /boot`. It is small and stays mounted when the array is stopped. Do not fill it with large files.

**Array, pools, shares.**

- The *array* is the parity-protected set of data disks: `/mnt/disk1`, `/mnt/disk2`, ... with up to two parity disks that have no mount point.
- *Pools* (cache and others) are separate devices or groups: `/mnt/<poolname>`. They can be btrfs, XFS or ZFS.
- *User shares* are the merged view at `/mnt/user/<share>`: one directory tree that spans the disks and pools included in that share. Files physically live on exactly one disk or pool; the share view is a FUSE overlay.
- The *mover* moves files between a pool and the array on a schedule according to each share's cache setting.

The single most important data rule: **never mix `/mnt/user/...` and `/mnt/diskN/...` (or `/mnt/<pool>/...`) in one copy or move command.** The user-share view and the underlying disk can refer to the same file; copying between them can truncate it to zero bytes. Pick one namespace per command.

**Array state.** `/var/local/emhttp/var.ini` is the live state file written by `emhttpd`. The lines that matter:

```
grep -E '^(mdState|fsState|mdResync|mdResyncAction|mdResyncPos|mdResyncSize|mdNumInvalid|mdNumMissing|mdNumDisabled|sbSynced|sbSyncErrs|shareMoverActive)=' /var/local/emhttp/var.ini
```

- `mdState=STARTED` and `fsState=Started`: array up, shares mounted.
- `mdState=STOPPED`: `/mnt/user`, `/mnt/disk*` and pools are gone; Docker and VMs are down.
- `mdResync` greater than 0: a parity check or rebuild is running; `mdResyncAction` says which, `mdResyncPos`/`mdResyncSize` give progress.
- Whether the array is *degraded* is decided per slot in `disks.ini`, the same way the webGUI does it: a `status` containing `_MISSING`, `DISK_DSBL` or `DISK_INVALID`, or a `color` starting with `red-` or `yellow-`, means a missing, disabled or emulated disk. Say so before doing anything else. Do not use the `mdNumInvalid`/`mdNumDisabled`/`mdNumMissing` counters for this: an empty parity slot (`status=DISK_NP_DSBL`, `color=grey-off`, no `device`) raises them on a perfectly healthy array.
- `/proc/mdstat` on Unraid is not the usual Linux format; it is the same `key=value` list as the `md*`/`sb*` keys in `var.ini`.

Other state files in `/var/local/emhttp/`: `disks.ini` (per-disk device, temperature, error counts, filesystem, free space, and the GUI `color`: `green-on` normal, `grey-off` empty or spun-down slot, `blue-*` new, `yellow-*` emulated or warning, `red-*` disabled or error), `shares.ini` (per-share settings and usage), `network.ini`, `users.ini`, and a cached SMART report per slot in `smart/<slot>`. They are INI files; read them with `grep`/`awk`, do not write them.

**The webGUI and emhttpd.** The webGUI (`/usr/local/emhttp`, nginx + PHP) talks to `emhttpd`, which owns the array. Starting or stopping the array, assigning disks, formatting, parity operations and share settings all go through it. Scripts that back the GUI live in `/usr/local/emhttp/webGui/scripts/` and `/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/dynamix/scripts/`; a few are safe and useful from the shell (see section 6).

## 2. What you must never do, and what needs consent

**Never, under any instruction:**

- New Config, clearing or formatting a disk, `mkfs`, `wipefs`, `sgdisk --zap`, `blkdiscard`, `zpool destroy`, `zpool labelclear`. The owner does these in the webGUI, deliberately.
- Edit `/boot/config/super.dat`, `disk.cfg`, `pools/`: these are the disk assignments. A wrong edit means the array will not start or parity is rebuilt over good data.
- Read or print password hashes, keys, tokens, WireGuard private keys, API keys. The conversation is stored in plain text and synced off the machine.
- Stop the array with `mdcmd stop` or unmount disks by hand. If the array must stop, the owner does it from Main.
- Edit `/boot/grub/grub.cfg` or create `/boot/syslinux/syslinux.cfg` on an Internal Boot system. Boot parameters go through Main -> Flash -> Boot Parameters (backed by `manage_boot_params.sh`). The GUI's CPU-pinning code, the OS updater and `diagnostics` all look for `/boot/syslinux/syslinux.cfg` first and fall back to `grub.cfg`; a stray `syslinux.cfg` makes them write kernel options into a file GRUB never reads.

**Explain the impact and get an explicit yes, one action at a time:**

- Starting or stopping the array; rebooting or shutting down.
- Starting, pausing or cancelling a parity check; any rebuild.
- Changing disk assignments, adding or removing pool devices.
- Deleting a container, VM, share or appdata directory; removing a plugin.
- Updating Unraid itself or any plugin.
- Anything that writes under `/boot/config` (back it up first, see section 3).
- Running a scrub or balance on a pool (long, I/O heavy).

For everything else, prefer the read-only check first, then the smallest change that does the job.

## 3. Changing configuration

**Back up before you touch `/boot/config`.** Copy the file to `/boot/steward/backups/<YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS>/` keeping the relative path, then edit. Tell the owner what changed, how to restore it, and whether a service restart or reboot is needed.

**Three tiers of files under `/boot/config`:**

| Tier | Files | How to change |
|---|---|---|
| Meant for hand editing | `go`, `go.safemode` (optional), `stop`, `udev/*.rules`, `modprobe.d/*.conf`, `smb-extra.conf`, `network-extra.cfg`, `editor.cfg`; with the third-party User Scripts plugin also `plugins/user.scripts/scripts/` | Edit with a backup. Unraid reads them; the webGUI does not rewrite them. |
| Rewritten by the webGUI | `ident.cfg`, `network.cfg`, `share.cfg`, `shares/*.cfg`, `docker.cfg`, `domain.cfg`, `plugins/dockerMan/templates-user/my-*.xml`, `plugins/dynamix/dynamix.cfg`, `plugins/dynamix/monitor.ini`, `plugins/dynamix/*.cron` | Use the webGUI when there is a page for it. A hand edit works until the next Apply on that page, and it does not take effect until the matching service reloads. `shares/*.cfg` has no command-line reload at all: share settings are webGUI only. |
| Never by hand | `super.dat`, `disk.cfg`, `pools/`, `passwd`, `shadow`, `smbpasswd`, `secrets.tdb`, `ssh/`, `ssl/`, `wireguard/`, `*.key`, `machine-id`, `network-rules.cfg` | Only through the webGUI, or not at all. |

Notes:

- `rc.local` runs `go` through `fromdos` first, so Windows line endings are tolerated there. Other files are not so forgiving.
- Unraid's `rc.local` may itself rewrite `go` (it strips certain lines and leaves `go~` behind). A change to `go` in the Flash Backup history is not necessarily human.
- Scripts kept on `/boot` should be run as `bash /boot/path/script.sh` rather than relying on the execute bit.
- Cron: drop a file into `/boot/config/plugins/dynamix/<name>.cron` and run `/usr/local/sbin/update_cron`. It concatenates those files (plus the `.cron` files of installed plugins) into the single crontab `/etc/cron.d/root`, so anything else placed in `/etc/cron.d` is discarded on the next run, and `crontab -e` edits RAM.

**Services.** Most are SysV-style under `/etc/rc.d/`: `rc.samba`, `rc.nfsd`, `rc.docker`, `rc.libvirt`, `rc.nginx`, `rc.sshd`, `rc.ntpd`, `rc.avahidaemon`. Use `restart` or `reload` sparingly and say which clients will be interrupted. After editing `smb-extra.conf` by hand, `/etc/rc.d/rc.samba reload` regenerates the Samba configuration and reloads it without dropping sessions; `/etc/rc.d/rc.nfsd reload` does the same for NFS exports. `/usr/local/emhttp/webGui/scripts/reload_services` is not a general reload: it only runs when the webGUI has queued a job with `at`, and does nothing otherwise.

## 4. Docker

Containers on Unraid are defined by templates: `/boot/config/plugins/dockerMan/templates-user/my-<name>.xml`. The webGUI builds the `docker run` from the template, and only containers with a template can be edited, updated or re-created from the Docker page.

- Do not create long-lived containers with `docker run` by hand. They will work, but they have no template, so the owner cannot manage them in the webGUI and they are lost on the next "update all". Create them in the webGUI (Add Container, or Community Applications) or write a template and let the owner apply it.
- Read-only inspection is fine and useful: `docker ps -a`, `docker logs --tail 200 <name>`, `docker inspect <name>`, `docker stats --no-stream`, `docker image ls`, `docker system df`.
- Starting, stopping and restarting a container is a routine operation: `docker start|stop|restart <name>`. Say what depends on it first (reverse proxy, databases).
- Removing containers, images, volumes or networks needs consent. `docker system prune` and `docker volume rm` destroy data.
- Updates: the Docker page's "check for updates" and Community Applications handle image updates so the template stays in sync. `docker pull` by hand leaves the webGUI showing stale state.
- appdata lives in the share set by `docker.cfg` (usually `/mnt/user/appdata` on a pool). Treat it as the container's database: back it up before changing it, never `chmod -R` it.
- The Docker image or directory itself (`docker.cfg`: `DOCKER_IMAGE_FILE`) must not be deleted or resized by hand.

## 5. Virtual machines

libvirt manages VMs; definitions are in `/etc/libvirt/qemu/*.xml`, persisted to the libvirt image on the pool. `virsh list --all`, `virsh dominfo <vm>`, `virsh domstats <vm>` are safe. `virsh shutdown <vm>` is a graceful request and acceptable with consent; `virsh destroy <vm>` is a hard power-off and `virsh undefine <vm>` deletes the definition: both need explicit consent. VM disks (vdisks) live under the domains share; never delete or shrink them.

## 6. Useful commands that are safe

```
cat /etc/unraid-version                                   # OS version
grep -E '^(mdState|fsState)=' /var/local/emhttp/var.ini   # array state
df -h /boot /var/log                                      # boot device and the RAM log partition
free -m; uptime                                           # memory, load
zpool status; zpool list                                  # ZFS pools (if any)
btrfs filesystem show; btrfs device stats /mnt/<pool>     # btrfs pools
smartctl -n standby -H -A /dev/sdX                        # SMART without spinning up a sleeping disk
tail -n 200 /var/log/syslog                               # system log (RAM; the previous boot's log is /boot/logs/syslog-previous)
diagnostics                                               # anonymised diagnostics zip into /boot/logs (never -a without the owner)
command grep -P '...' file                                # GNU grep; plain "grep" inside Claude Code is a ugrep shim with pattern limits
/usr/local/emhttp/webGui/scripts/notify -e "Steward" -s "<subject>" -d "<text>" -i normal   # Unraid notification
```

Things that look harmless but are not:

- `mdcmd` anything: it is the array control interface. Even `mdcmd status` is fine, but it is simpler to read `/proc/mdstat` or `var.ini`. Never `mdcmd stop`, `mdcmd nocheck`, `mdcmd check` without consent.
- `smartctl` without `-n standby` spins up sleeping disks. `hdparm -C /dev/sdX` shows the power state without waking it.
- `du` or `find` across `/mnt/user` spins up every disk and can take hours. Ask before scanning whole shares; prefer `shares.ini` for usage numbers.
- A shell whose working directory is under `/mnt` prevents the array from stopping. Use absolute paths and keep `cd` out of `/mnt`.

## 7. Files you create on shares

New files under `/mnt/user/...` are owned by root and SMB users cannot open them. After creating files there: `chown nobody:users <files>` and `chmod ug+rw <files>`, only on the files you created. Never run `chown -R` or `chmod -R` on appdata, system, domains or a whole share; the Unraid "New Permissions" tool exists for that and the owner runs it. Avoid `cp -a`/`rsync -a` from root-owned sources into shares for the same reason.

## 8. Logs and diagnostics

- `/var/log/syslog` is in RAM and limited in size. If `/var/log` is full (`df -h /var/log`), something is logging in a loop; find it with `ls -laS /var/log` before anything else.
- By default the syslog is copied to the boot device at shutdown and shows up as `/boot/logs/syslog-previous` after the next boot. Continuous mirroring to the boot device or a remote server is a separate setting under Settings -> Syslog Server.
- `diagnostics` writes `/boot/logs/<host>-diagnostics-<date>.zip`. The default run anonymises IPs, user names and registration data; `diagnostics -a` does not. Tell the owner before sending it anywhere.
- Container logs: `docker logs`. VM logs: `/var/log/libvirt/qemu/<vm>.log`. Mover: enable mover logging in Settings -> Scheduler, then grep syslog for `move:`.
- When a report says "disk error", look at `disks.ini` `numErrors` and the SMART attributes (5, 187, 197, 198, 199) before concluding anything. Read errors during a parity check are logged as `md: disk<N> read error` lines.

## 9. The Unraid API

Unraid 7.2 and later include a GraphQL API with a CLI: `unraid-api --help`. Docs: https://docs.unraid.net/API/. Creating an API key is `unraid-api apikey --create`: ask the owner first, use the narrowest role that works, store the key at `/boot/steward/home/unraid-api.key` with mode 600, and never print it. The API is the right tool when you need structured data (array, disks, shares, containers, notifications) rather than parsing INI files.

## 10. Reporting back

Lead with the state that matters (array degraded, disk errors, pool near full), then what you did, then what you did not do and why. For anything long-running or anything that now needs a decision, send an Unraid notification so the owner sees it without opening the conversation. Keep numbers in a short table. Say plainly when something could not be verified.
]]></INLINE>
</FILE>

<!-- 7. Install (also runs on every boot) -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE><![CDATA[
# Reached on every boot (then it only prepares; the service starts in the background) and when installing or updating from the webGUI
exec 2>&1
bash /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/steward/scripts/steward.sh _plugin_install
]]></INLINE>
</FILE>

<!-- 8. Remove: stop the service and take out the hooks. Data in /boot/steward is kept -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE><![CDATA[
exec 2>&1
s=/boot/steward/steward.sh
[ -f "$s" ] || s=/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/steward/scripts/steward.sh
if [ -f "$s" ]; then bash "$s" _plugin_remove; fi
rm -rf /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/steward
exit 0
]]></INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
